{"id": "d6cb893b-5678-43d7-90d7-f6cfffafb248", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:06:13.729911", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "41961d4b-97b0-4d34-adca-276306dc4246", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Confirmed: GPUs physically present on host (GB100/B100 class per lspci) but not usable — nvidia-smi fails, no /dev/nvidia* device nodes, torch not installed, no network. So no local execution possible.\n\nNow I'll write the scoping plan. Let me be careful about the technical facts I state about BAdam and LoRA, marking them as recalled-literature assumptions:\n\nBAdam (Blocked Approximation (actually \"Blocked Total Active-Discarded Model\" — BAdam: Efficient Low-rank Fine-Tuning of LLMs with Blocked Block-Coordinate Descent, ICLR 2024, Sun et al., arXiv 2312.05685). Mechanism: it starts from a LoRA-style low-rank decomposition of the weight update (frozen base weights + low-rank matrices A, B), but instead of updating all low-rank parameters each step, it partitions the parameter indices into blocks and, at each step, activates only one block (or a small subset) of low-rank parameters via block-coordinate descent, discarding/deactivating others. Because only a small slice of the parameters is active, optimizer states (Adam moments) are only allocated for the active block, so per-step activation/optimizer memory is much lower and constant regardless of model size. This is \"activation sparsity of parameter updates\" — the sparse-update mechanism. The name BAdam = \"Blocked Adam\"? Actually paper: \"BAdam: Efficient Low-rank Fine-tuning of Large Language Models with Blocked Block-Coordinate Descent\". Reported: fine-tune LLaMA-33B on a single RTX 3090 24GB. They report comparable or better performance vs LoRA while drastically reducing memory.\n\nImportant note: BAdam's memory win comes primarily from updating a tiny fraction of low-rank params per step (so optimizer states and, crucially, backprop through most layers can be skipped for inactive blocks). In BAdam, inactive blocks are not updated so their gradients need not be computed — this reduces backprop memory/compute (they avoid computing gradients for inactive parameters), which yields the throughput advantage.\n\nLoRA baseline: Hu et al. 2021; apply to all linear projections (q,k,v,o,gate,up,down) with rank r; the standard configuration in MathInstruct papers... Actually the MathInstruct paper itself (MathInstruct: Improving Mathematical Reasoning through Instruction, AAAI 2024) used Llama-2-70B finetuned with 240k synthetic instruction-solution pairs, and reported GSM8K 61.9% (8-shot) and MATH 33.6% (4-shot? actually 4-shot). Their finetuning was full finetuning of Llama-2-70B I believe (with some frozen layers). Note: the question asks about Llama 3-70B + MathInstruct, which resembles the LLaMA-3 tech report setup (Llama 3 used ~100k math samples generated from Llama-2-70B MathInstruct pipeline; Llama-3-70B-Instruct achieved GSM8K 93.0 and MATH 50.4 zero-shot). Also relevant: Meta used MathInstruct-style data for Llama 3.\n\nFor the design: benchmarks — GSM8K, MATH (test), plus optionally: MMLU-STEM/math subset, MathQA, SVAMP, MultiArith, AQuA, GSM-Hard, Olympiad-level? Keep primary: GSM8K + MATH (MATH500 subset). Secondary: GSM-Hard, SVAMP, as robustness. And contamination handling: n-gram overlap detection between test problems and MathInstruct training set (MathInstruct was generated from source datasets including GSM8K train, MATH train, etc. — indeed MathInstruct's 240k pairs derive from 13 source datasets: NumGLUE, MathQA, GSM8K, MATH, NumGLUE, Unimath, TheoremQA, etc.), so train/test contamination must be handled by de-duplicating training pairs whose question matches test problems (n-gram/ exact-match on the answer + question).\n\nZero-shot definition: prompt with instruction only, no exemplars; answer extraction via \\boxed{} / \"final answer is\" regex; grading by math equality (sympy / math_verify normalization). Also report 4-/8-shot as secondary because MATH is hard zero-shot, and because the Llama 3 instruction model was trained with some prompt style; so we should define the primary as zero-shot with a fixed instruction template, and report few-shot sensitivity.\n\nFairness axes: trainable parameter budget matching — LoRA rank r for all linear layers; BAdam also uses a low-rank base of the same total rank so the number of trainable low-rank parameters is identical; BAdam activates only a fraction ρ per step (e.g. block size chosen so active fraction ≈ matches...). Then a second arm with LoRA on same rank (full-update), and optionally a \"compute-matched\" or \"optimizer-step-matched\" variant: BAdam with k passes to equalize number of *active parameter updates*. Also a third control: full-parameter/QLoRA? Keep: LoRA (primary baseline), BAdam (method), plus controls: LoRA with matched active-update budget (i.e., LoRA with same learning-rate/step budget), plus frozen base (no-tune) reference, plus optionally full finetune if compute allows.\n\nKey confound: BAdam's effective per-step update magnitude differs; must match total \"update volume\" = trainable params × optimizer steps. And BAdam's original paper had a controversial \"bug\" (the BAdam repo was reportedly temporarily pulled in early 2024 due to an issue that inflated results, later fixed) — I should flag that as a literature-memory uncertainty to verify, and require pinning to a specific commit/version.\n\nAlso: with only a small active block, BAdam may need more epochs. Report per-epoch curves.\n\nStatistics: ≥3 seeds (5 if budget allows), primary endpoint = mean of GSM8K+MATH exact-match accuracy averaged across seeds; paired comparisons per seed; report mean ± SD, 95% CI via bootstrap over test items; test with paired permutation test on per-item correctness or a mixed model with seed random effect; equivalence margin; decision rule: claim BAdam \"better\" only if lower bound of 95% CI for Δ (BAdam − LoRA) exceeds +0.5 pt on average of two primary benchmarks (i.e., exceeds MCID) across seeds; otherwise declare null/tie; also require the seed-level direction consistency (e.g., ≥4/5 seeds). Secondary endpoints: cost metrics with pre-registered hypotheses (BAdam expected lower peak memory and higher tokens/sec).\n\nAlso handle memory-vs-accuracy decoupling: pre-specify four-quadrant outcome reporting.\n\nResource unknowns: which GPU cluster (8×A100/H100 80GB?), whether Llama 3-70B weights are accessible under Meta license, whether the environment here has no network/GPU (confirmed), runtime budget.\n\nFeasibility notes: 70B full model bf16 ≈ 140 GB weights; LoRA with FSDP/ZeRO-3 across 8×80GB, sequence packing at 2k/4k context, batch size, ~240k samples × 1-3 epochs. Estimate ~ compute: 70B LoRA on 240k samples of ~700 tokens → roughly 240k*700 = 168M tokens per epoch; with 6*P*T FLOPs for fwd+bwd-ish (with LoRA only activations grad flows through, so still need full forward + backward through activations: ~ 2*P*T forward + ~ 2*P*T backward w.r.t. activations) → ~ 4*70e9*1.68e8 = 4.7e19 FLOPs/epoch ≈ 47 EFLOP... let me compute: 4 * 70e9 * 1.68e8 = 4.7e19. At 300 TFLOP/s effective per H100 bf16 × 8 GPUs = 2.4e15 FLOP/s → 1.96e4 s ≈ 5.4 h/epoch of pure compute, realistically 15-30 h/epoch with 30-40% MFU and comms. For 5 seeds × 2 arms × 3 epochs → very large: ~ 6 × 3 × 20h ≈ 360 GPU-hours... wait that's 8-GPU-hours. 360 hours × 8 GPUs = 2880 GPU-hours ≈ 120 days of one 8-GPU node. Too much. So budget: reduce seeds to 3, epochs to 1 (MathInstruct paper used 1 epoch I think), and/or subsample data. Flag this: total ≈ 1800–3000 8-GPU-hours at full scale; recommend a staged design (pilot at 7B/8B scale with identical recipes to screen, then 70B confirmatory with 3 seeds) and/or reduce MathInstruct to a fixed 60k subsample for the seed sweep.\n\nGood — a staged design: Stage 0 pilot at Llama-3-8B (cheap, validates pipeline, ranks), Stage 1 confirmatory at 70B with 3 seeds.\n\nNow let me write the plan handoff. It should be thorough but a plan skeleton, plus assumptions and uncertainties. Include the environment verification evidence.\n\nI'll structure the answer:\n1. Environment verification results\n2. Approach skeleton (a)-(e)\n3. Assumptions & uncertainties\n4. Open questions for the researcher (limited to consequential ones).\n\nNote question_budget 6 — I can ask questions in the handoff.\n\nLet me double-check a couple of numbers I quote as estimates and mark them explicitly as back-of-envelope arithmetic, not measurements. I can compute them with execute_check for accuracy.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "python3 - <<'PY'\nP=70e9\nsamples=240_000; avg_tok=700\ntok_per_epoch=samples*avg_tok\nflops=4*P*tok_per_epoch   # fwd + bwd-through-activations approx\nprint(\"tokens/epoch %.2e\"%tok_per_epoch)\nprint(\"FLOPs/epoch %.2e\"%flops)\nfor name,mfu,gpus,peak in [(\"8xA100-80G\",0.35,8,312e12),(\"8xH100-80G\",0.4,8,989e12)]:\n    eff=gpus*peak*mfu\n    h=flops/eff/3600\n    print(f\"{name}: wall-clock/epoch ~{h:.1f} h (8-GPU node-hours)\")\nprint(\"3 seeds x 2 arms x 1 epoch, 8xH100:\", round(3*2*flops/(8*989e12*0.4)/3600,1),\"node-hours\")\nprint(\"bf16 weights GB:\", 2*P/1e9)\nprint(\"fp8/4bit GB:\", 1*P/1e9)\n# LoRA trainable params estimate for 70B, r=16 on all 7 linears\n# 70B: 80 layers, d_model=8192; q=8192x8192, k/v=1024x8192 (GQA), o=8192x8192, mlp: gate/up 8192x28672, down 28672x8192\nr=16; d=8192; kv=1024; f=28672; L=80\nper_layer=2*r*( (d*d)+(kv*d)+(kv*d)+(d*d) + (d*f)*3 )\nprint(\"LoRA r=16 trainable params ~%.2e (%.2f%% of 70B)\"%(per_layer*L, 100*per_layer*L/P))\nr=64\nper_layer64=2*r*( (d*d)+(kv*d)+(kv*d)+(d*d) + (d*f)*3 )\nprint(\"LoRA r=64 trainable params ~%.2e (%.2f%%)\"%(per_layer64*L, 100*per_layer64*L/P))\nPY", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b13ec20c4d3471ea", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-b13ec20c4d3471ea", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Compute rough compute/memory estimates for feasibility sketch\", \"command\": \"python3 - <<'PY'\\nP=70e9\\nsamples=240_000; avg_tok=700\\ntok_per_epoch=samples*avg_tok\\nflops=4*P*tok_per_epoch   # fwd + bwd-through-activations approx\\nprint(\\\"tokens/epoch %.2e\\\"%tok_per_epoch)\\nprint(\\\"FLOPs/epoch %.2e\\\"%flops)\\nfor name,mfu,gpus,peak in [(\\\"8xA100-80G\\\",0.35,8,312e12),(\\\"8xH100-80G\\\",0.4,8,989e12)]:\\n    eff=gpus*peak*mfu\\n    h=flops/eff/3600\\n    print(f\\\"{name}: wall-clock/epoch ~{h:.1f} h (8-GPU node-hours)\\\")\\nprint(\\\"3 seeds x 2 arms x 1 epoch, 8xH100:\\\", round(3*2*flops/(8*989e12*0.4)/3600,1),\\\"node-hours\\\")\\nprint(\\\"bf16 weights GB:\\\", 2*P/1e9)\\nprint(\\\"fp8/4bit GB:\\\", 1*P/1e9)\\n# LoRA trainable params estimate for 70B, r=16 on all 7 linears\\n# 70B: 80 layers, d_model=8192; q=8192x8192, k/v=1024x8192 (GQA), o=8192x8192, mlp: gate/up 8192x28672, down 28672x8192\\nr=16; d=8192; kv=1024; f=28672; L=80\\nper_layer=2*r*( (d*d)+(kv*d)+(kv*d)+(d*d) + (d*f)*3 )\\nprint(\\\"LoRA r=16 trainable params ~%.2e (%.2f%% of 70B)\\\"%(per_layer*L, 100*per_layer*L/P))\\nr=64\\nper_layer64=2*r*( (d*d)+(kv*d)+(kv*d)+(d*d) + (d*f)*3 )\\nprint(\\\"LoRA r=64 trainable params ~%.2e (%.2f%%)\\\"%(per_layer64*L, 100*per_layer64*L/P))\\nPY\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-770b782f-ccbb-91f8-beaa-8dd3dee8a3e8", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Compute rough compute/memory estimates for feasibility sketch", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
