{"id": "f8043374-22fb-49e2-87cd-baed913d4b01", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:09:20.091578", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "28afc43b-ccab-40d9-81db-83ee8eee6019", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Good. These are analytic estimates (arithmetic), fine to present as back-of-envelope, not measurements.\n\nNote that optimizer-state memory at r=64 is only 6.6GB — the real BAdam memory win in the paper is about activation/backprop memory: by freezing inactive blocks, the backward graph through most transformer blocks can be truncated, drastically reducing activation memory (this is the mechanism I should attribute, flagged as literature-recall assumption to verify). I should note this design nuance: to reproduce the claimed memory win, the implementation must skip backward for layers where no trainable block is active; otherwise BAdam degenerates to LoRA with fewer updates and no memory benefit. This is a consequential design detail (a \"mechanism fidelity check\" as an internal validity control).\n\nNow write the plan handoff. Keep it organized and reasonably compact but complete at skeleton level. Include explicit questions for researcher (consequential choices needing approval).\n\nLet me draft.\n\nEvidence section: list of environment observations with commands.\n\nI'll write the plan with clear structure. Then end_researcher_turn stage=plan.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "## 1. Scoping / inspection results (verified, nothing executed as an experiment)\n\n**Workspace is empty of research inputs.** `/workspace` contains exactly one file: `task.json` (613 bytes), which holds only the question text plus the note `\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"` There is no repository, no source code, no data files.\n\n**Confirmed NOT locally available** (explicitly, so nothing is implied to have been run/downloaded):\n- **MathInstruct** (nvidia/MathInstruct, ~240k instruction–solution pairs): absent. `find / -maxdepth 3 -iname \"*mathinstruct*\"` → no hits; `datasets`/`huggingface_hub` not installed; no `~/.cache/huggingface` directory at all.\n- **Llama 3-70B weights** (or any checkpoint): absent. No `*llama*` artifacts on disk; 1 TB of `/` is tmpfs with 0 bytes of model content.\n- **BAdam reference implementation and any LoRA/PEFT reference implementation**: absent. `torch`, `transformers`, `peft` all raise `ModuleNotFoundError`; `pip list` shows only system/Ansible-type packages; no `*badam*`/`*lora*` paths found.\n- **No usable GPU**: `nvidia-smi` binary exists but returns *\"NVIDIA-SMI has failed because it couldn't communicate with the NVIDIA driver\"*; there are **no `/dev/nvidia*` device nodes** (only `/dev/nvidia-caps`). `lspci` shows GB100-class 3D controllers on the *host*, but they are **not exposed into this bubblewrap sandbox**, and `gpu_enabled: false` in the task spec. I will not claim any accelerator is usable here.\n- **No network**: DNS resolution fails (`socket.gaierror` to pypi.org) → no pip install, no dataset/model download. Python is 3.14.4 (also too new for the usual CUDA torch wheels, a secondary practical blocker).\n- Host-scale CPU/RAM exists (224 CPUs, ~2 TB RAM) but is irrelevant to 70B finetuning.\n\n**Consequence for the deliverable:** this is a pure *design* artifact. Any design I write must be specified so that a separate, GPU-equipped environment could execute it, with all quantitative statements labelled as analytic estimates/arithmetic rather than measurements.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Proposed approach — skeleton of the experimental design\n\n### (a) Method definitions and the mechanisms being compared\n- **Base model (both arms):** Llama 3 70B (institution: Meta Llama 3 70B base checkpoint, bf16). Same checkpoint hash for both arms; tokenizer/chat template fixed. *Assumption A1:* the **base** (non-instruct) checkpoint is used so that finetuning is the only treatment; using `Llama-3-70B-Instruct` would confound the treatment with prior RLHF math tuning.\n- **BAdam arm:** I will attribute to BAdam the mechanism from Sun et al. (ICLR 2024, \"BAdam: Efficient Low-rank Fine-Tuning of LLMs with Blocked Block-Coordinate Descent\"): a LoRA-style low-rank reparameterisation of the weight update on frozen base weights, combined with **blocked block-coordinate descent** — the low-rank parameter indices are partitioned into blocks and, at each optimisation step, only **one block of the low-rank parameters is activated** and updated by Adam; all other low-rank parameters receive no update that step. Crucially, the claimed memory/throughput advantage requires that **inactive blocks do not participate in backprop**, so activation/optimizer-state memory becomes independent of model size. I will add an explicit **mechanism-fidelity probe** (a hard internal-validity check): verify by instrumentation that (i) per-step active-parameter fraction matches the configured block ratio, (ii) Adam state is allocated only for the active block, (iii) the autograd graph is actually truncated for inactive layers. If (iii) fails, BAdam degenerates to \"LoRA with sparser updates\" and the memory claim cannot be tested — this must be reported, not silently averaged.\n- **LoRA baseline:** standard LoRA (Hu et al. 2021) with **all** low-rank parameters active every step, applied to **the same 7 linear projections per transformer block** (q, k, v, o, gate, up, down), same `alpha`/scaling convention, same initialisation semantics, base weights frozen.\n- **Rank matching:** identical low-rank geometry in both arms (same target modules, same rank r). Default r = 16 → ~207 M trainable params ≈ 0.30 % of 70 B (my own arithmetic from Llama-3-70B dims: 80 layers, d=8192, kv=1024, f=28672). Sensitivity arm at r = 64 (~828 M, 1.18 %).\n\n### (b) Fairness / equity axes to control (pre-registered)\n1. **Trainable-parameter budget** — identical (same r, same modules); BAdam differs *only* in the fraction activated per step.\n2. **Update-sparsity / effective-update budget** — the axis the question silently hides. Two pre-registered matching regimes, and I will report both:\n   - *Parameter-matched* (primary): both arms see the same number of optimiser steps and same data; BAdam therefore performs strictly fewer parameter updates.\n   - *Update-volume-matched* (secondary): BAdam steps × active-ratio ≈ LoRA steps × 1, i.e. BAdam runs extra passes/steps so total active-parameter updates are equal. Without this, a BAdam loss can be an artefact of undertraining rather than of the mechanism.\n3. **Optimizer** — AdamW everywhere (β1=0.9, β2=0.95, wd=0.0, ε=1e-8), same fp32 master-state dtype for active params.\n4. **LR + schedule** — same peak LR and the same *shape*; cosine, warmup = 3 % of steps. Because sparse updates change effective step size, I will additionally run a **small LR sensitivity grid** for the parameter-matched comparison so neither arm is strawmanned, and pre-register \"best LR per arm within a declared grid\" as the fairness criterion.\n5. **Epochs / steps / data ordering** — identical epoch count and identical shuffled sample order per seed (seed-derived permutation shared across arms); same sequence packing, same drop-last policy; gradient accumulation and effective global batch (tokens) identical; same max sequence length (2 048 default, 4 096 sensitivity) so packing cannot differ.\n6. **Seed set** — identical seed list, and no seed-cherry-picking.\n7. **Precision / kernels** — bf16 autocast, TF32 on, FlashAttention on, same gradient-checkpointing setting (reported separately for the memory metric because checkpointing choice dominates peak memory).\n8. **Parallelism/IO** — identical FSDP/ZeRO-3 sharding plan, same node count, same micro-batch, same storage backend and warm page cache state for both arms.\n9. **Rejection-sampling / filtering** — none applied to either arm; identical preprocessing (chat template, loss mask on prompt tokens, solution tokens supervised).\n\n### (c) Evaluation protocol\n- **Primary benchmarks (true zero-shot):** GSM8K test (1 319) and MATH test (5 000, plus the MATH500 subset for cheap iteration). Rationale: they are the two benchmarks on which both the MathInstruct paper and the Llama 3 report report, so the result is comparable to existing literature; they also span arithmetic-word-problem vs olympiad-style difficulty.\n- **Secondary benchmarks:** GSM-Hard (robustness to perturbation), SVAMP and MultiArith (narrow-skill generalisation), MMLU-STEM/math subset and AQuA (transfer), TheoremQA (hard ceiling). Pre-registered: primary conclusion comes only from GSM8K+MATH.\n- **Zero-shot definition (explicit, to remove ambiguity):** single instruction prompt, **no worked exemplars in the context**, no chain-of-thought trigger phrases beyond a fixed neutral instruction, greedy decoding (temperature 0), max generation 1 024 tokens, no external calculator/tool use, no majority voting/self-consistency, no answer-checker reranking. Few-shot (4-shot and 8-shot, fixed exemplar sets, identical across arms) is reported as a **secondary** sensitivity, because MATH zero-shot accuracy is low and a null zero-shot result could be a floor artefact.\n- **Answer extraction/scoring:** deterministic two-stage scorer — (1) format-aware extraction (last `\\boxed{...}`, then \"final answer is/=\" pattern, then last numeric expression), (2) mathematical-equity grading via symbolic normalisation (sympy/`math_verify`-style), string-normalised for multiple choice. Extraction failures are counted **incorrect** and reported per arm (differential format failure is itself an outcome, not noise). Scorer is frozen and unit-tested on 200 hand-labelled exemplars before any arm is scored.\n- **Contamination handling:** MathInstruct pairs are generated from source datasets that include GSM8K and MATH *train* splits, so leakage risk is real. Pre-registered procedure: (i) exact-match and 8-gram/char-n-gram Jaccard overlap of every MathInstruct question against **all test sets** (threshold declared in advance), (ii) flag-and-report the overlap rate for both splits, (iii) primary analysis on the **decontaminated** test subset (test items with no flagged training counterpart), with the full test set as a sensitivity analysis; (iv) also run an untrained base-model reference to quantify how much each benchmark is \"already solved\" pre-treatment.\n- **Leakage guards:** benchmarks are read-only, checksummed, and never used for LR selection or early stopping; model selection is done on a held-out slice of MathInstruct only.\n\n### (d) Cost metrics (BAdam's claimed advantage lives here)\nReported per arm, per seed, from instrumented runs: **peak per-GPU memory** (allocated and reserved, at identical checkpointing settings, plus the theoretical activation-memory prediction), **training throughput** (tokens/s and samples/s at steady state, excluding first 5 % of steps), **step time**, **end-to-end wall-clock** to the accuracy endpoint, **total GPU-hours**, **checkpoint storage** (bytes written, adapter-only vs optimizer-state bytes), **data/IO volume and dataloader wait fraction**, and **evaluation cost** (generation tokens, GPU-hours). Memory is decomposed into weights / activations / gradients / optimizer state so a memory delta can be *attributed* to a component rather than asserted.\n\n### (e) Statistical analysis plan\n- **Seeds:** 3 confirmatory seeds (5 if budget approved) per arm per condition; unit of replication is the run, not the test item.\n- **Primary endpoint:** mean exact-match accuracy across GSM8K + MATH, per run. **Δ = BAdam − LoRA** computed per matched seed (paired).\n- **Inference:** paired bootstrap over test items nested within seeds for 95 % CIs; seed-level direction consistency; a mixed-effects model (fixed: arm, benchmark, matching regime; random: seed) as the confirmatory model. Item-level McNemar/permutation tests are **descriptive only** — they ignore seed variance and would overstate significance.\n- **Decision rule (pre-registered):** BAdam is declared \"better on zero-shot math\" only if the 95 % CI lower bound for Δ exceeds a pre-registered minimum relevant difference of **+0.5 absolute points** on the pooled primary endpoint **and** the direction is consistent in ≥ 3 of 3 (≥ 4 of 5) seeds. If the CI lies within ±0.5 pt, the outcome is declared **equivalent/undetermined**, not \"LoRA better\". A win on memory alone is reported in its own quadrant and never written as \"better performance\".\n- **Pre-registered 4-quadrant reporting:** accuracy-win+cost-win / accuracy-win+cost-neutral-or-worse / accuracy-null+cost-win / accuracy-null+cost-neutral — with a template sentence for each, so a null result is publishable by construction.\n- **Multiplicity:** Holm correction across the 2 primary benchmarks and the 2 matching regimes; secondary benchmarks labelled exploratory.\n- **Feasibility sizing (arithmetic, not measurement):** ~1.68e8 supervised tokens/epoch for 240k samples at ~700 tokens; ≈ 4.7e19 FLOPs/epoch (4·P·T) → ~4 h/epoch on an 8×H100 node at 40 % MFU, ~15 h/epoch at A100-class 35 % MFU; 3 seeds × 2 arms × 1 epoch ≈ 25–90 8-GPU-node-hours, and ~3× that with the update-matched regime and r=64 sensitivity. bf16 weights alone are 140 GB, so multi-GPU sharding is mandatory for both arms; the illustrative optimizer-state figures at r=64 are ~6.6 GB (all-active Adam moments) vs ~0.83 GB at a 1/8 active ratio, which shows why activation/backprop memory — not optimizer state — must be the component I attribute any memory win to. A **stage-0 pilot at Llama-3-8B** with the identical recipe is proposed first to validate plumbing and the mechanism-fidelity probe at ~1/70 the cost, before spending any 70B budget.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Consequential assumptions and open questions for you (all marked as assumptions, not facts)\n\nAssumptions (A-list, to be confirmed or overridden):\n- **A1 Base vs Instruct checkpoint:** using Llama 3 70B **base**; if you want the Instruct checkpoint, the whole prompt/extraction section changes.\n- **A2 \"Zero-shot\" = no in-context exemplars, greedy, no tools/self-consistency.** If the intended convention is the Llama-3-report convention (a particular prompt/instruction-tuning format, or \"0-shot CoT\"), the primary metric must be restated.\n- **A3 BAdam mechanism attribution** as blocked block-coordinate descent over LoRA-style low-rank params with inactive blocks excluded from backprop; version/commit to be pinned (I recall the public BAdam repo had an early-2024 correctness issue that was fixed post-release — this is a recollection to be **verified against the source**, not a claim, and it is exactly why the fidelity probe is in the design).\n- **A4 Parameter-matched is the primary comparison** and update-volume-matched is secondary; if the reviewer community for this question expects compute-matched primary, that inverts the primary/secondary labelling.\n- **A5 MathInstruct = full 240k pairs, 1 epoch, no resampling**; a subsample (e.g. fixed 60k) is my fallback if the seed budget is tight.\n- **A6 Only linear-projection adapters** (no IA³/prefix/soft-prompt arms, no full finetuning arm) — full finetuning is out of scope at 70B under any plausible budget; a frozen-base reference run is included instead.\n- **A7 Feasibility unknowns that would change the design:** (i) which GPUs and how many (8×A100-80G vs 8×H100 changes the design from \"3 seeds, 1 epoch\" to \"3 seeds, 3 epochs\"), (ii) wall-clock budget in GPU-hours, (iii) whether Llama 3-70B weights are licensable/downloadable in the executing environment, (iv) whether eval-only serving of a 70B model is available for the benchmark sweep (≈ hundreds of GPU-hours for greedy generation over MATH+GSM-Hard+MMLU at 1k-token outputs), (v) storage for multiple 140 GB checkpoints plus optimizer state, (vi) persistent (non-tmpfs) scratch space. **None of these are known here**, so the design will be written with a staged \"minimum viable / full\" tier structure and explicit stop/go gates rather than a single fixed budget.\n- **A8 Null-result handling:** pre-registered as above — an accuracy tie is reported as a tie with an equivalence statement; a memory-only win is reported as a cost result that does **not** answer \"better performance\" affirmatively. No post-hoc seed or benchmark selection.\n\nQuestions I need answered before writing the full design (I'll keep to these; they are the load-bearing ones):\n1. **Base or Instruct** Llama 3-70B checkpoint?\n2. **Which matching regime is primary** — parameter-matched (default proposal) or update/compute-matched?\n3. **Resource envelope** of the intended executing environment (GPU type × count, GPU-hour budget, eval-serving availability) — or should I write it as tiered minimum/full designs with no stated budget?\n4. **Benchmark scope confirmation:** GSM8K + MATH as primary with GSM-Hard/SVAMP/MMLU-math as exploratory, or a different required list?\n\nNo experiment has been run, and nothing has been downloaded or measured beyond the environment probes above.", "evidence": "Environment inspection (all commands run in the sandbox; exploratory only, no experiment executed):\n\n1. `ls -la /workspace` / `find /workspace -maxdepth 3` → only `task.json` (613 B). `cat /workspace/task.json` → contains only the question, the context line \"Do not claim that an experiment was run\", and `documents.track.md: \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"` No code, no data.\n\n2. Python/ML stack: `python3 -V` → Python 3.14.4. `python3 -c \"import torch\"` → `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'torch'`; same for `transformers` and `peft`. `pip list` shows only system packages (ansible, cryptography, jinja2, …) — no ML/datasets/hub libraries. No `*badam*`, `*lora*`, `*mathinstruct*`, `*llama*3*` paths found by `find / -maxdepth 3`. No `~/.cache` or `~/.cache/huggingface` directory exists → no cached model/dataset artifacts.\n\n3. GPU: `nvidia-smi` exists at /usr/bin/nvidia-smi but prints \"NVIDIA-SMI has failed because it couldn't communicate with the NVIDIA driver\". `ls /dev/nvidia*` → \"no /dev/nvidia* devices\" (only /dev/nvidia-caps). `lspci` lists host GB100 [TS4/B100] 3D controllers, but they are not exposed in this bubblewrap sandbox; task spec also states `gpu_enabled: false`. Conclusion: no accelerator usable here.\n\n4. Network: `socket.create_connection(('pypi.org',443))` → `socket.gaierror: [Errno -3] Temporary failure in name resolution`. Consistent with the spec's `network: disabled`. No install/download is possible.\n\n5. Host resources (not sufficient for the task): `nproc` = 224; `free -g` = ~2011 GB RAM; `/` and `/tmp` are tmpfs with ~1006 GB free and 0 bytes used.\n\n6. Analytic sizing (arithmetic computed locally, NOT measurements): 240k samples x ~700 tok = 1.68e8 tokens/epoch; 4*P*T ≈ 4.70e19 FLOPs/epoch → ~4.1 h/epoch on 8xH100 at 40% MFU, ~15.0 h/epoch on 8xA100 at 35% MFU; 3 seeds x 2 arms x 1 epoch ≈ 25 node-hours (H100) to ~90 node-hours (A100-class). bf16 weights = 140 GB. LoRA trainable params from Llama-3-70B dims (80 layers, d=8192, kv-head dim 1024, ffn 28672, 7 target linears/block): r=8 → 103.5 M (0.148%), r=16 → 207.1 M (0.296%), r=32 → 414.2 M, r=64 → 828.4 M (1.183%); all-active Adam fp32 moments at r=64 ≈ 6.63 GB vs ≈ 0.83 GB at a 1/8 active ratio — i.e. optimizer-state savings are single-digit GB, so any large memory win must come from truncated backprop/activations, which motivates the mechanism-fidelity probe in the plan.", "assumptions": ["Llama 3 70B **base** (not Instruct) checkpoint is the finetuning starting point; using Instruct would confound the treatment with prior math RLHF.", "'Zero-shot' is defined as no in-context exemplars, greedy decoding, no CoT trigger beyond a neutral instruction, no tools, no self-consistency voting; few-shot results are secondary sensitivities.", "BAdam is attributed the blocked block-coordinate-descent mechanism over LoRA-style low