{"id": "512b2341-1eda-426a-8927-c5039d0bd365", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:09:53.580052", "source": "user", "parent_id": "83e31bf0-550e-4d49-85ad-255b5a01aebf", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved — the scoping is credible (empty workspace, no ML stack, no exposed GPU, no DNS) and I agree this must be written as a pure design artifact with every quantitative statement labelled analytic arithmetic rather than measurement. Nothing has been run, and I do not want any wording that implies otherwise. Your mechanism-fidelity probe (does the implementation actually truncate the backward graph for inactive blocks?) is the single best idea in the plan — keep it as a hard gate, not a footnote. Please now write the full design. Answers to your four load-bearing questions, stated as my directives for this study (not as facts about the source systems):\\n\\n1) Checkpoint: use the Llama 3 70B **base** checkpoint as primary (your A1), for the confounding reason you gave. Do not spend design effort on an Instruct arm; instead write a one-paragraph external-validity note on what would change if a practitioner started from Instruct, since that is the realistic deployment case.\\n\\n2) Matching regime: keep **parameter-matched as primary**, but do not let update-volume-matched sit in the appendix — promote it to a **co-primary secondary** with its own declared decision role, i.e. the accuracy claim for BAdam is only defensible if the parameter-matched result is corroborated (or explained) by the update-matched result. State explicitly, in the results template, that a parameter-matched BAdam deficit accompanied by an update-matched parity is evidence of *undertraining*, not of a harmful sparsity mechanism. Cost metrics stay descriptive (not a primary endpoint) — your quadrant scheme is the right way to keep a memory win from being written up as \\\"better performance\\\".\\n\\n3) Resources: I cannot give you a GPU type, node count, or GPU-hour envelope, and I do not want you to assume one. Write the **tiered structure** you proposed (stage-0 Llama-3-8B plumbing/fidelity pilot → minimum-viable 70B comparison → full design with r=64 and the LR grid), each tier with explicit stop/go gates, the decision each gate unlocks, and the arithmetic that scales with (GPU class × count × MFU assumption). Keep your FLOPs/memory arithmetic but present it in a clearly-labelled \\\"analytic estimates, not measurements\\\" table, and make the eval-serving cost a first-class line item rather than an afterthought — you are right that greedy generation over MATH + GSM-Hard + MMLU-math at ~1k output tokens can rival training cost, and that changes the budget materially.\\n\\n4) Benchmarks: confirmed. GSM8K + MATH as the primary zero-shot endpoints (with MATH500 as a cheap development subset only, never for selection), GSM-Hard/SVAMP/MultiArith/MMLU-math/AQuA/TheoremQA as exploratory. Keep your few-shot (4/8-shot) arm purely as the floor-artefact sensitivity, and keep the frozen, unit-tested scorer with extraction failures counted incorrect and reported per arm.\\n\\nThree things I want tightened while writing, all identifiable in your own plan rather than new demands:\\n- **Contamination power analysis.** You flag that MathInstruct derives partly from GSM8K/MATH train sources and that the decontaminated primary population may be smaller, but you do not say what happens if overlap is large. Specify the pre-registered fallback: what overlap rate triggers switching the primary population back to the full test set (with the contamination rate disclosed), and what minimum decontaminated item count you require before you consider the decontaminated analysis adequately powered given your ±0.5 pt minimum relevant difference.\\n- **Block-ratio / active-fraction as a declared hyperparameter, not a constant.** Your design fixes \\\"same r, same modules\\\" but BAdam's per-step active-block ratio is the knob that trades accuracy for memory. Declare it as a small pre-registered grid, state which setting is the headline configuration and on what basis it is chosen (paper default vs a development-slice choice on MathInstruct only), and make clear it must not be chosen using any benchmark.\\n- **Seed count honesty.** Three seeds with a paired 95 % CI against a 0.5 pt threshold is thin. State the detectable-effect reasoning for 3 vs 5 seeds analytically, and let the tier gates decide the count, rather than presenting 3 as sufficient by default.\\n\\nAlso please carry your assumptions list into the deliverable as a labelled A-list with a \\\"how this design would break if the assumption is wrong\\\" column, including the unverified recollection about the BAdam repo's early correctness fix — keep it explicitly marked as unverified and to be checked against the source, and keep it tied to the fidelity probe. Deliverables required: final experiment design with rationale, controls, analysis plan, and feasibility, plus a results/reporting template that makes a null or memory-only result reportable by construction. Still no execution claims, no fabricated numbers, and no fabricated citation details — if you cite mechanism specifics to Sun et al. (ICLR 2024) or Hu et al. (2021), keep them at the level you can defend and flag anything version-dependent.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "79638222-c3f1-408d-a6c4-b21357e8972a", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:11:16.409866", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "512b2341-1eda-426a-8927-c5039d0bd365", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Approved — before writing, let me nail down the arithmetic that the three requested tightenings depend on (contamination power floor, seed-count detectable-effect reasoning, eval-serving cost), so the deliverable contains no hand-waving numbers."}], "reasoning_content": "Now I need to write the full design deliverable. Let me do a bit more arithmetic to support the contamination power analysis and seed-count detectable-effect reasoning, then write the full design.\n\nLet me compute:\n1. Detectable effect: paired difference on mean accuracy across seeds. Variance components: seed-to-seed run variance (unknown, assume σ_seed) and item-level binomial. For n items, SE of accuracy ~ sqrt(p(1-p)/n). For GSM8K p=0.5, n=1319 → SE=1.38pt; MATH p=0.2, n=5000 → SE=0.566pt. Pooled (average of two benchmarks): SE = 0.5*sqrt(SE1²+SE2²)... Actually the average of two accuracies: Var = (Var1+Var2)/4 → SE = sqrt(SE1²+SE2²)/2 = sqrt(1.38²+0.566²)/2 = sqrt(1.90+0.32)/2 = sqrt(2.22)/2 = 1.49/2 = 0.745 pt. Hmm — item-level sampling error alone (0.75 pt) exceeds the 0.5 pt MRD! That's important and honest: with a single fixed test set, item sampling error is not reducible by more seeds; item-level bootstrap CI on a fixed test set gives the CI conditional on those items, but generalization to a new test item population would require more items. Important nuance: the primary inference target. Two inference targets: (a) the fixed test set performance of the trained models (finite, so item-level uncertainty is about population generalization), (b) the training-procedure distribution across seeds.\n\nKey honest point: paired comparison across the SAME items removes item-level sampling variability from the *difference* estimate to the extent both arms err on the same items —