milar since eval scales with models too. So eval share is ~12-15% roughly invariant. Where does eval blow up? With max_new_tokens 4096 for open-ended sets: multiplier on 9.49M → let's compute: long sets items with 1024: MATH 5000, GSM8K 1319, GSMHard 1319, TheoremQA 800 = 8438 items → ×4 = +25.3M tokens; per model becomes 9.49+25.3 = 34.8M (zero-shot) and with few-shot ~ (34.8 + 2*6.47)= 47.7M → ×5 → 0.33B tokens. FLOP-based: full battery 4× on long sets → total FLOPs = tot(1.757) - prim_long_part... let me just state multipliers: raising the generation cap from 1k→4k tokens on open-ended sets multiplies eval cost ~3.4×, i.e. 8xH100: 3.9→~13 node-h; 8xA100: 12.4→~42 node-h, which approaches a single 70B epoch (89.7 node-h on A100s). That's the honest version of \"eval can rival training\".\n\nI'll compute these multipliers quickly? I can reason: eval FLOPs = prefill + decode. Decode portion for long sets dominates. tot = 1.757e18, of which MATH+GSM8K+GSMHard+TheoremQA decode = 2*70e9*(5000+1319+1319+800)*1024 = 1.4e11*8438*1024=1.21e18. Quadrupling decode on those: +3×1.21e18 = +3.63e18 → new tot ≈ 5.39e18 (3.07×). With few-shot primaries: full = tot + 2*prim where prim decode = 2*70e9*6319*1024=0.906e18, prim prefill small. full_new ≈ 5.39 + 2*(0.906*4 + small) ≈ 5.39+7.3 ≈ 12.7e18 → 3.2× of 3.99. So 7 models: 88.9e18 vs 27.95e18 → 3.2×. At 8xA100 with MFU 0.25: 12.4×3.2 = 39.7 node-h. Yes ≈40 node-h, comparable to ~44% of one A100 epoch and clearly material. Good.\n\nNow, let me also make sure about the \"min decontaminated item count\" spec: I'll define the power target operationally: the decontaminated analysis must keep the item-level 95 % half-width ≤ 1.5 pt on the pooled endpoint (given assumed discordance pd=0.15), which requires retained fraction f ≥ 0.61 → n_g ≥ ~810 and n_m ≥ ~3075 (rounded thresholds: n_GSM8K ≥ 800, n_MATH ≥ 3000). Pre-registered rule: if the flagged-overlap rate exceeds 39 % on either primary test set (i.e., retained < thresholds), the primary population reverts to the full test set with the contamination rate disclosed in every table, and the decontaminated analysis becomes a sensitivity analysis. Also require reporting contamination rate, and a \"leak-restricted\" contrast: run both arms on a *decontaminated training set* (MathInstruct pairs flagged as overlapping test items removed) at the minimum-viable tier with k seeds — that's the clean fix: train on decontaminated MathInstruct so the primary population IS the full test set and no power is lost. That's the better design choice: do the decontamination on the TRAINING side, not the test side! Yes — the cleanest: remove overlapping MathInstruct pairs from training for both arms (identical filter), so the full test set remains valid and powered. Test-side removal then becomes a redundancy check for near-duplicates that the training filter missed. I should highlight this as the primary approach — it dissolves the power problem. But we don't know a priori the overlap rate; the training-side filter costs at most a few % of the 240k training pairs. Excellent tightening. I'll present: (1) training-side filter as primary (both arms identical filter, filter is benchmark-blind... well it's benchmark-aware but arm-blind), (2) test-side decontaminated subset as the confirmatory sensitivity with the pre-registered power floor and reversion rule.\n\nNow let me also handle block-ratio grid declaration:\n- Declared knob: active-block ratio ρ (fraction of low-rank params active per step) or equivalently block count B = 1/ρ.\n- Pre-registered grid: ρ ∈ {1/64, 1/16, 1/4} — hmm, what's the paper default? I recall the BAdam paper used something like \"1/2^k blocks\" and activated one block per step; for LLaMA-33B they activated a block of size... I honestly don't recall the exact default. I must NOT fabricate citation specifics. I'll say: the headline ρ is taken from the reference implementation's default if it can be verified from the source (to be recorded in the pinned-commit record); if the default cannot be verified, headline ρ = the setting on the pre-registered grid that matches the paper's reported active-parameter count per step *if documented*, else the middle grid point, chosen on a MathInstruct development slice only (never on benchmarks), with the tie-break rule declared in advance (lowest ρ whose dev loss within 0.02 nats of the best ρ, to favour the memory-advantageous setting).\n\nGrid: ρ ∈ {1/2, 1/8, 1/32} maybe. I'll define the grid as {1/1, 1/4, 1/8, 1/32}? ρ=1 is LoRA-equivalent (all active) which is the LoRA arm itself — good anchor. Let me declare ρ ∈ {1/4, 1/8, 1/32} with the note that ρ=1 reduces to LoRA and is not run as a BAdam config. Headline = ρ=1/8? I'll pre-register dev-slice selection among {1/4, 1/8, 1/32} using a MathInstruct held-out slice (dev loss + a dev-only 500-item math set drawn from MathInstruct train only — never a benchmark). Also declare that ρ must be identical across seeds, and that each ρ gets its own memory row, so we can plot the accuracy–memory frontier instead of a single point. Also require reporting the frontier: the primary comparison is at the headline ρ, but the frontier is what supports/undercuts the mechanism claim.\n\nUpdate-volume matching math: for ρ=1/8, BAdam needs 8× steps to match total active-parameter updates → that's 8× the *parameter-update* count, but wall-clock per step is lower (the claimed efficiency). Actually BAdam's per-step wall-clock is roughly similar or lower; the honest way to define update-matched: run BAdam for S/ρ steps at ratio ρ (matching active-element-updates) — this multiplies the number of forward/backward passes by 1/ρ, which at ρ=1/8 means 8× the data passes → that's 8 epochs vs 1 epoch. Cost explodes. Alternative: match total update *volume* by keeping steps equal but scaling ρ... no, that's the parameter-matched arm.\n\nHmm. Important design decision: how to implement update-volume matching affordably. Options:\n(a) Run BAdam for