run when the gate budget allows. Both framed as the undertraining diagnostic. Good — this respects the researcher's directive while being feasible.\n\nNow the seed-count gate: pre-register that σ̂_run is estimated from tier 0/tier 1 repeats, and the confirmatory k is chosen by a pre-registered rule: k = smallest of {3,5,7} such that t_{k-1}·σ̂_run/√k ≤ (target halfwidth H), where H is set so the smallest claimable Δ̂ ≤ 1.5 pt (given MRD 0.5): H=1.0 → with σ_run=0.8, k=7 (0.74); σ_run=1.2 → k=7 (1.11) → not met at k=7 → then k=9. And explicitly state: if σ̂_run ≥ 2.0 pt, no seed count within budget can support a 0.5 pt claim; the design then reports the effect size with its CI and declares an *undetermined* result rather than increasing seeds indefinitely. That's honest seed-count honesty.\n\nAlso: the item-level term is ~1.2-1.7 pt at plausible discordance, which means even with infinite seeds the 0.5 pt MRD cannot be cleared at MATH-scale item counts unless discordance is low. This is a crucial, honest finding: I must state that the ±0.5 pt claim threshold is only reachable when paired discordance is low (≲5%) or when the effect is larger than ~1–1.7 pt. Therefore pre-register: the claim-warranting threshold on the pooled endpoint is +0.5 pt MRD, but the *design's honest minimum detectable difference* (MDD) is computed from measured item discordance and σ̂_run and reported alongside — and the results template's decision rule uses the CI lower bound vs MRD, so the design will simply report \"undetermined\" when MDD > observed Δ. Also state the option to reduce item noise by increasing effective item count — impossible without new benchmarks; so the practical implication is that this study can only claim a win if it is ≥ ~1.5–2 pt. Excellent honest framing.\n\nAlso note the discordance assumption is measurable at stage 0 (8B) and from pilot runs; and the pooled-endpoint discordance term uses per-set pd; also the two arms' predictions across seeds are clustered (same items across seeds), so I'll note using a two-way/mixed model with item random effects + seed random effects rather than naive item bootstrap; nested bootstrap: resample seeds and items.\n\nNow: deliverables:\n1. Final experiment design (methods, arms, controls, protocol).\n2. Rationale.\n3. Controls table.\n4. Analysis plan (incl. power/seeds, contamination, decision rules).\n5. Feasibility: tiered with gates + arithmetic table.\n6. Results/reporting template with quadrants and undertraining language.\n7. A-list assumptions with break-mode column.\n8. External validity note on Instruct.\n9. Citation defensibility statement.\n\nThis is going to be long, but that's the deliverable. Let me write it carefully in the handoff `answer` field, and put environment/repo observations + arithmetic in `evidence`.\n\nAlso need: block-ratio grid; fidelity probe as hard gate with specific tests; eval-serving first-class line item.\n\nLet me define arms concretely:\n\nArms/configs (per tier):\n- A0: Frozen base (no training) reference.\n- A1: LoRA (parameter-matched primary), ρ=1, r=16.\n- A2: BAdam @ headline ρ (parameter-matched primary).\n- A3: LoRA-pass-matched control: LoRA trained on S·ρ steps (equal forward/backward passes to A2) — tests harm at equal passes.\n- A4: BAdam @ ρ with update-matched schedule (1/ρ passes) — co-primary secondary (or A3 as affordable proxy).\n- A5: BAdam @ off-grid ρ values (frontier).\n- A6: LoRA r=64 (sensitivity), BAdam r=64.\n- LR grid: {lr_base, 2×, 0.5×} per arm on development slice only for selection; confirmatory at selected LR.\n\nCareful: the \"best LR per arm within a declared grid\" selection is on the MathInstruct dev slice (held-out 1000 pairs, loss-based + dev math accuracy proxy from MathInstruct held-out items). Pre-register the selection criterion: lowest held-out MathInstruct loss (token-level), tie-break by dev accuracy.\n\nAlso declare the loss mask, sequence packing, template.\n\nAlso: pre-registration artifacts: the design should be registered as a frozen config file with a hash before running; a \"pre-registration bundle\" listing config hashes.\n\nAlso: reporting: extraction-failure rates per arm; format-adherence differences; and a \"format-parity\" check that both arms produce comparable answer formats (a known confound: different finetuning schemes produce different answer formatting; if BAdam produces less parseable output, accuracy gap is partly format). Control: a format-normalized scoring variant where an LLM-free normalizer extracts, plus reporting accuracy conditional on parseable output (with counts). Good control to include.\n\nAlso: a \"same-step-count vs same-token-count\" note. And the checkpoint-selection rule: final checkpoint at end of epoch (no early stopping on benchmarks), plus intermediate checkpoints evaluated at fixed intervals for the learning curve (descriptive).\n\nNow the fidelity probe specifics (hard gate, tier 0):\nG1: active-parameter census — instrument the optimizer to log, per step, the set of parameter slices updated; assert |active|/|total trainable| ∈ ρ ± 0.01, and blocks cycle with period 1/ρ; assert coverage uniformity (each parameter active in the same number of steps over a full cycle, χ² test).\nG2: gradient-graph truncation — compare peak activation memory at fixed micro-batch with BAdam ρ=1/8 vs LoRA; expect a large reduction if truncation happens. Direct test: register hooks on base-model layers to count backward invocations per layer; assert that for blocks with no active adapter, the backward function is not invoked (or that grad is not requested for the input activation). If base-model params are frozen, then a base layer whose downstream trainable params are all inactive in the current step need not compute grad-inputs — the check is that the number of backward kernel calls scales with the number of active blocks.\nG3: memory attribution — per-component memory accounting (weights/activations/grads/optimizer) via torch memory snapshots at identical micro-batch, seq len, checkpointing settings; assert optimizer-state memory scales with active block size.\nG4: numerical equivalence sanity — at ρ=1 (all active) with the same LR, BAdam's update must reduce to Adam-on-LoRA; assert loss curves match LoRA within tolerance. This catches the kind of bug behind early-2024 reports. If ρ=1 does NOT reproduce LoRA, the implementation is suspect → gate fails → pin/patch/choose alternative.\nThat G4 is a beautiful gate tied to the unverified recollection.\n\nGate structure:\n- G0 (environment/legitimacy): weights + data obtained, hashes, licenses; scorer unit test ≥95% agreement with 200 hand labels.\n- G1 (fidelity, 8B): probes G1-G4 pass.\n- G2 (pipeline, 8B): both arms' dev curves behave; σ̂_run measured from 3 seeds; measured step-time ratio r_t = t_BAdam/t_LoRA → decides update-matched feasibility.\n- G3 (minimum viable 70B): 1 seed smoke test at 70B; memory fits; throughput measured; then k chosen by rule.\n- G4 (confirmatory): run k seeds, analysis per plan.\n- G5 (full design): r=64, LR grid, frontier, few-shot, exploratory battery.\n\nEach gate: decision unlocked, arithmetic scaling formula.\n\nCost table: I'll include per-config node-hour formulas: node-hours ≈ 4·P·T/(G·peak·MFU·3600).\n\nLet me include the formula so it scales with any GPU class × count.\n\nNow writing. I'll aim for a well-organized long answer. Let me be careful with citation defensibility: mention \"Sun et al., ICLR 2024 (BAdam)\" and \"Hu et al., 2021 (LoRA)\" at mechanism level only, flag arXiv IDs as to-be-verified? I'd better not give arXiv IDs since I'm not fully certain. Actually I recalled 2312.05685 — that seems right for BAdam, but the instruction says no fabricated citation details; I'll say \"identifier to be confirmed\" if I mention it, or just avoid IDs. I'll avoid IDs entirely and note \"pinned commit/version record required\".\n\nLet me write the final deliverable now. I'll keep tables compact using plain text/pipe tab