 items... no, keep: option to raise MRD is not allowed post hoc. Instead: state the resolution achieved and label claims accordingly. Also state a \"power gate\": measure discordance at the 8B stage-0 pilot and at 70B seed-pair, then compute the achieved resolution and pre-register it in the analysis lock before unblinding outcomes — but discordance is only observable after seeing both arms' outputs... which is outcome data. Handling: discordance between arms is outcome-dependent. Solution: estimate it from the stage-0 8B run and from the frozen-base-vs-arm discordance, and lock the projected resolution from stage-0 only, never from 70B outcomes. That's a clean rule.\n\nSeed count honesty: 3 vs 5 vs 7 given s_run ∈ {0.8, 1.2, 2.0} — the arithmetic table D. Rule: choose k by budget and by whether seed term is ≤ 50% of item term (diminishing returns); recommend k=5 if affordable else k=3 with explicit statement that the 0.5pt claim is unreachable and the design's honest output is equivalence/undetermined. And an adaptive variance-based (arm-blind) seed addition rule.\n\nContamination floor: from script C: at pd=0.15, retaining fraction f≥0.96 keeps halfwidth ≤1.2pt... Actually the printed numbers: pd=0.15 → halfwidth ≤1.5pt needs f≥0.61 (n_g≥811, n_m≥3075); pd=0.20 → f≥0.82 (n_g≥1081, n_m≥4100); pd=0.10 → f≥0.41 for 1.5pt.\nPre-registered rule: primary population = decontaminated subset if retained fraction f ≥ 0.60 for BOTH test sets AND absolute counts n_g ≥ 800, n_m ≥ 3000 (which keeps 95% halfwidth ≤1.5pt under pd≤0.2 assumptions — hmm at pd=0.2, f=0.61 gives hw 1.75. Let me set the trigger as: f≥0.80 AND n_g≥1050, n_m≥4000 → hw ≤1.52 at pd=0.2, ≤1.07 at pd=0.1). That's the cleanest: threshold f≥0.80. If overlap removal drops below 80% retention on either set, switch primary population back to the FULL test set with contamination rate disclosed in the headline, plus report the decontaminated analysis as sensitivity, plus a matched-subset analysis (restricted to items retained, applied to both arms — always report both). Also: report the overlap rate itself (unknown until measured), and note it's likely non-trivial since MathInstruct pairs were generated from math-problem datasets (mechanism-level claim, flagged as recollection to verify).\n\nAlso add the \"answer-level\" contamination: MathInstruct derived from *train* splits → test sets should be safe by construction if the benchmark train/test split is respected; the risk is near-duplicate problems (GSM8K has known near-duplicates). So specify two-stage detection: exact numeric-normalized question match, and token-level near-duplicate via MinHash/Jaccard on numerics-masked text with pre-registered threshold 0.85 on masked 5-gram containment, plus a stricter 0.6 sensitivity. Also note the base model (Llama 3) pretraining corpus may itself contain benchmarks — uncontrollable; handled by including the frozen-base reference and reporting deltas-from-base rather than absolute accuracies (important!). Good: make Δ-from-base a reported quantity, and note the *difference-in-differences* logic: base model contamination affects both arms identically (same base), so the paired arm-vs-arm comparison is robust to pretraining contamination; contamination in MathInstruct affects both arms identically too (same data), so it biases absolute numbers upward but affects the *comparison* only if the two arms memorize leaked items differently — which is a real asymmetry hypothesis (BAdam, being less-updated, may memorize less; that's a *confound in the direction of favoring LoRA*, worth naming). Add a \"leak-responses\" analysis: compute the arm difference separately on leaked vs clean items — if the arms differ only on leaked items, interpret as memorization not reasoning.\n\nBlock ratio / active fraction: declared grid ρ ∈ {1/16, 1/8, 1/4} of low-rank params active per step (equivalently blocks b = 16, 8, 4 cycles), plus the paper's default if determinable. Headline rule: (i) if the reference implementation's published default is verifiable, use it as headline; else headline = ρ chosen on a MathInstruct-only development slice (2k held-out pairs, loss-based) at the *largest* accuracy-per-memory knee — with explicit prohibition on benchmark use; (ii) all three ratios reported, headline pre-specified, and the accuracy–memory Pareto curve is the primary methodological artifact for the cost axis. Also require: BAdam's block schedule must complete a full cycle (all blocks visited) a whole number of times within the training run — pre-registered so both arms end \"equally trained\" in coverage terms; choose total steps as multiple of cycle length; state the update-volume matching in these terms (update-matched = BAdam steps × ρ ≈ LoRA steps, achieved by integer cycle counts).\n\nAlso note the interaction: because the effective update budget is steps×ρ, parameter-matched comparison at low ρ is likely undertrained → hence co-primary update-matched arm is not optional. State the arithmetic: at ρ=1/8, update-matched BAdam needs ~8 epochs of passes to equal active-parameter updates → 8× cost unless paired with larger ρ or fewer LoRA steps; define update-matched practically as: BAdam runs N_c complete block cycles such that total active-block-steps = LoRA steps; wall-clock per step may be lower (that's the claim), so the update-matched arm's cost is 1/ρ × per-step-time-ratio × LoRA cost. Give the formula: C_match ≈ C_LoRA × (1/ρ) × τ where τ = BAdam step time / LoRA step time (expected τ<1; the design's cost claim is essentially whether τ·(1/ρ) ≤ 1 for equal update volume). Nice, that's a crisp formulation. So the update-matched arm doubles as the honest cost-matched comparison. Pre-register: if ρ = 1/8 and τ ≥ 0.5, update-matched costs ≥4× → declare that arm budget-capped and use ρ = 1/2 as the update-matched headline for that arm (fewer cycles). Good: make ρ_for_matching a declared choice: run update-matched at the largest ρ in the grid so cost stays feasible.\n\nStatistical model: mixed-effects logistic on item-level correctness (arm, benchmark fixed; random intercept seed + item), or two-stage: per-seed accuracy → paired differences → t-based CI with k-1 df, plus item bootstrap within run. Pre-registered primary estimator: seed-level paired t (hierarchical, since seed is the unit that varies the training procedure), with item-level bootstrap CIs as secondary. Emphasize: item-level bootstrap alone understates uncertainty (treats training as deterministic).\n\nAlso add: no early stopping, final checkpoint only (plus fixed-interval checkpoints for curves, all evaluated, labeled as descriptive); pre-registered \"no benchmark peeking\" with eval logs hashed.\n\nTiers:\n- Tier 0 (8B plumbing + fidelity, ~3 node-h at 8×H100-equivalent): all code paths, fidelity probe G1/G2/G3, scorer unit test, extraction-failure baseline, discordance estimate for power lock, LR grid smoke, memory scaling sanity. Gates: G0 code hash + scorer ≥95% agreement on 200 hand-labelled items; G1 active-fraction instrumentation matches configured ρ; G2 Adam state allocated only for active block; G3 backward-graph truncation confirmed (activation memory scales with active layers) — FAIL ⇒ memory hypothesis untestable, report mechanism-fidelity failure, do not proceed to 70B cost claims (may still proceed for accuracy-only comparison with a sparsity-only label).\n- Tier 1 (minimum viable 70B): parameter-matched only, single LR, headline ρ, k=2 seeds for variance estimate + memory; gate: runs complete without divergence; σ̂_run measured → feeds seed sizing.\n- Tier 2 (full): k seeds (5 preferred, ≥3), parameter-matched + update-matched arms, ρ grid, r=64 sensitivity, LR grid ±half/double, few-shot sensitivity, exploratory battery.\nEach gate lists the decision it unlocks.\nScaling formula table: node-hours ≈ (4·P·T_tokens)/(N_gpu·peak·MFU·3600); eval ≈ (2·P·T_gen)/(N_gpu·peak·MFU_decode·3600). Give a matrix of GPU classes with the MFU assumptions clearly marked as *assumptions to be replaced by measured values at Tier 0/1* and no GPU commitment assumed.\n\nReporting template: results table skeleton, quadrant sentences, undertraining sentence, mechanism-fidelity-failure sentence, contamination-fallback sentence, seed-insufficiency sentence.\n\nA-list with break-mode column — required.\n\nCitation defensibility: keep mechanism claims at the level \"as described in the public BAdam paper/implementation (Sun et al., ICLR 2024) and LoRA (Hu et al., 2021)\"; state no citation details beyond author/venue/year were verified here; unverified recollection flagged.\n\nLength: needs to be complete but readable. I'll use compact tables. Let me write it.\n\nAlso: \"Do not spend design effort on an Instruct arm; one-paragraph external-validity note.\" OK.\n\nLet me now write the final answer. I'll keep it organized with headers. Aim for thorough but not bloated; maybe 2000-2600 words equivalent. Given it's the deliverable, thoroughness is expected.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9682044e029bca29", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-9682044e029bca29", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# EXPERIMENT DESIGN — BAdam vs LoRA on Llama 3 70B + MathInstruct, zero-shot math transfer\\n\\n**Status / provenance.** Design-only artifact. Nothing was trained, downloaded, or measured. Every number below is either (i) a *direct sandbox probe* (E-numbered, in Evidence), or (ii) **analytic arithmetic** under explicitly declared assumptions (T-numbered). No GPU was used. Citations are limited to mechanism-level attribution (author/venue/year) with version-dependent details flagged as unverified.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Question, hypotheses, endpoints\\n\\n**Primary question.** Does BAdam beat LoRA on zero-shot math when finetuning Llama 3 70B base on MathInstruct?\\n\\n- **H1 (accuracy, primary, parameter-matched):** pooled zero-shot accuracy of BAdam > LoRA.\\n- **H2 (corroboration, CO-PRIMARY SECONDARY, update-volume-matched):** under equal *active-parameter-update volume*, BAdam ≥ LoRA.\\n- **H3 (cost, descriptive only, never a claim of \\\"better performance\\\"):** BAdam uses less peak memory and/or more tokens/s at equal parameter budget.\\n\\n**Endpoints.**\\n- Primary: `A_pooled = mean(GSM8K acc, MATH acc)` per run, on the pre-registered test population (§5).\\n- Co-primary secondary: same quantity in the update-matched regime.\\n- Descriptive: component-wise memory, throughput, wall-clock, storage/IO, extraction-failure rate, format-failure rate; exploratory benchmarks.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Arms and configurations\\n\\n| Arm | Method | Rank / modules | Active fraction per step | Role |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| A | LoRA (Hu et al., 2021) | r=16, all 7 linears/block (q,k,v,o,gate,up,down), α=32 | 1 (all params) | Primary baseline |\\n| B-hi / B-md / B-lo | BAdam (Sun et al., ICLR 2024) | identical to A | ρ = 1/4, 1/8, 1/16 | Treatment, ρ grid |\\n| A-lr | LoRA | r=16 | 1 | LR grid (½×, 1×, 2×) |\\n| B-um | BAdam, update-matched | identical to A | ρ_um = **1/2** (largest in grid; cost reason §7) | Co-primary secondary |\\n| C0 | Frozen base | — | — | Reference / floor |\\n| A64, B64 | LoRA / BAdam | r=64 | 1 / ρ_md | Sensitivity |\\n\\n**Mechanism attributed to BAdam** (defensible level): a LoRA-style low-rank reparameterisation of the weight update on frozen base weights, trained by **blocked block-coordinate descent** — low-rank parameters are partitioned into blocks and only one block (an ρ fraction of the low-rank parameters) is activated and Adam-updated per step. The claimed memory/throughput advantage additionally requires that **inactive blocks are excluded from the backward graph**; this is the design's central falsifiable engineering assumption, hence Gate G3 below.\\n\\n**ρ is a declared hyperparameter, not a constant.** Pre-registered grid ρ ∈ {1/16, 1/8, 1/4}; total steps are chosen so an integer number of complete block cycles fit in the run (coverage fairness: every low-rank parameter receives the same number of update slots by construction). Headline setting, in strict precedence: (1) the reference implementation/paper default *if verifiable against the source*; else (2) the knee of the accuracy–memory Pareto curve computed **only on a MathInstruct development slice** (2,000 held-out pairs, training loss + extractable-answer rate on held-out MathInstruct items); **never** on any benchmark. The full ρ curve, not a single point, is the methodological artifact for the cost axis.\\n\\n### 2.1 Mechanism-fidelity probe — HARD GATE (not a footnote)\\nRun at Tier 0 before any 70B spend.\\n- **G1 active-parameter census:** instrumented count per step of parameters receiving a nonzero update; must satisfy |active|/|trainable| = ρ ± 1 %, and block coverage must be uniform over a cycle (χ² check).\\n- **G2 optimizer-state allocation:** Adam moment buffers must exist only for active blocks (assert on allocation records, not on reported totals).\\n- **G3 backward-graph truncation (the decisive one):** with identical micro-batch, sequence length and gradient-checkpointing, compare (i) peak activation memory, (ii) count of autograd nodes / backward kernels, across ρ = 1, 1/2, 1/8, 1/16. Activation memory and node count must scale with the number of layers holding active blocks. **If G3 fails, the memory/throughput hypothesis is untestable as specified**: the run is reported as a *sparsity-of-updates* accuracy comparison with an explicit mechanism-fidelity-failure statement, and no cost claim of any kind is permitted.\\n- **G4 gradient-consistency sanity:** with ρ = 1, BAdam must reproduce LoRA's loss curve within tolerance (same seed, same data order). Failure ⇒ the implementation is not a superset of LoRA and the comparison is invalid; halt and re-pin the implementation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Controls (all pre-registered; the only intended difference is the update rule)\\n\\nIdentical for both arms: base checkpoint hash; tokenizer/chat template; train split and per-seed shuffled order (seed-derived permutation shared across arms); sequence packing and drop-last policy; max seq len (2 048 default, 4 096 sensitivity); global effective batch in tokens; optimizer AdamW (β1=0.9, β2=0.95, wd=0, ε=1e-8); LR peak and cosine shape with 3 % warmup; epoch/step count; loss mask (prompt masked, solution supervised); no resampling/filtering; bf16 autocast, TF32 on, FlashAttention on, **identical gradient-checkpointing**; identical FSDP/ZeRO-3 sharding plan, node count, micro-batch, storage backend and page-cache state. Fairness is additionally protected by declaring \\\"best LR per arm within the declared ½×/1×/2× grid, selected on the MathInstruct dev slice only\\\" as the comparison rule, so neither arm is strawmanned. No early stopping; final checkpoint is the evaluated artifact (fixed-interval checkpoints are evaluated for descriptive curves only). Eval logs are hash-logged to evidence the no-peeking rule.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Data and contamination protocol (pre-registered fallback)\\n\\nMathInstruct (~240k pairs) is the only training data; benchmarks are read-only, checksummed, never used for LR selection, checkpoint selection, or stopping.\\n\\n**Detection (run before training, frozen):** normalize questions (lowercase, strip punctuation, mask numerals), then flag a test item as *leaked-adjacent* if (i) exact match to a MathInstruct question, or (ii) ≥ 0.85 masked-5-gram containment (sensitivity: 0.60). Report overlap rates for GSM8K and MATH separately; these are currently **unknown** and must be measured, not assumed. Two-sided note: MathInstruct is reported to be generated from math datasets including GSM8K/MATH **train** splits (recollection, to verify), so test leakage should be near-duplicate rather than split inversion.\\n\\n**Pre-registered population rule (the tightened item):**\\n- Primary population = **decontaminated** test subset **iff** retained fraction ≥ **0.80 on both** GSM8K and MATH **and** n_GSM8K ≥ 1 050 and n_MATH ≥ 4 000.\\n- Otherwise the primary population **reverts to the full test set**, with the overlap rate disclosed in the headline table and the decontaminated analysis demoted to a sensitivity analysis.\\n- Rationale (arithmetic T5): with paired item discordance p_d, the pooled endpoint's item-level 95 % half-width is `0.5·√(p_d/n_g + p_d/n_m)`. At p_d = 0.15 the full sets already give ≈1.17 pt; dropping to 0.61 retention costs power that the 0.5 pt MRD cannot absorb. The 0.80/1050/4000 thresholds keep the item half-width ≤ ~1.5 pt even under the pessimistic p_d = 0.20.\\n\\n**Contamination logic that actually protects the comparison:** both arms see identical data and the identical base model, so any leakage inflates *both* arms. The asymmetric risk is differential memorisation (a sparsely-updated model may memorise leaked items less), which would bias **against BAdam**. Therefore: report Δ-from-base alongside arm-vs-arm, and report the arm difference separately on leaked-adjacent vs clean items; if the arms differ only on leaked-adjacent items, interpret as memorisation, not reasoning.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Evaluation protocol\\n\\n**Zero-shot (primary), stated to remove ambiguity:** one fixed instruction template, **zero exemplars**, no CoT trigger beyond the neutral instruction, greedy decoding (T=0, no sampling, no self-consistency, no tool/calculator use, no answer-checker reranking), max 1 024 new tokens, no context retrieval.\\n**Few-shot (4- and 8-shot, fixed exemplar sets, identical across arms) is a floor-artefact sensitivity only** — reported, never used for the headline claim, never for selection.\\n\\n**Primary benchmarks:** GSM8K test (1 319), MATH test (5 000). MATH500 is a development subset **only** (never for selection or reporting of the headline). **Exploratory:** GSM-Hard, SVAMP, MultiArith, MMLU-STEM/math, AQuA, TheoremQA.\\n\\n**Scoring (frozen before any arm is scored):** deterministic two-stage — (1) format-aware extraction (last `\\\\boxed{...}` → \\\"final answer is/=\\\" → last numeric expression); (2) mathematical-equity grading by symbolic normalisation (sympy-style), string match for MCQ. **Extraction failures are counted incorrect** and reported as a per-arm rate next to accuracy, because differential format breakdown is itself an outcome. The scorer is frozen, versioned, and unit-tested against 200 hand-labelled generations (Gate G0 requires ≥ 95 % agreement) before any arm is scored.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Cost metrics (descriptive, first-class)\\n\\nPer arm, per seed, instrumented: peak per-GPU memory decomposed into **weights / activations / gradients / optimizer state**; steady-state tokens/s (excluding first 5 % of steps); step time; wall-clock to the accuracy endpoint; GPU-hours; checkpoint bytes (adapter vs optimizer state) and write/read IO volume and dataloader wait fraction; **eval-serving cost as its own line item** (§8). Memory deltas are only reported *with* their component attribution — an unattributed memory delta is not reported. Gradient checkpointing is a declared constant, not a free variable, since it dominates peak memory.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Statistical analysis plan and honest power\\n\\n**Estimator (primary, pre-registered):** seed-level paired analysis. Δ_s = A_pooled(BAdam, s) − A_pooled(LoRA, s) over matched seeds; mean Δ̂ with a t-based 95 % CI on k−1 df; plus a mixed-effects logistic model on item-level correctness (fixed: arm, benchmark, regime; random: seed, item) as the model-based confirmation. **Item-level bootstrap CIs are secondary and explicitly labelled as understating uncertainty**, because they treat training as deterministic and ignore seed variance; McNemar/permutation tests are descriptive only.\\n\\n**Seed-count honesty (arithmetic T4, no claim that k=3 suffices).** The CI half-width from the seed term is `t_{k−1}·ŝ_run/√k`, and a superiority claim over the MRD = 0.5 pt requires Δ̂ > 0.5 + that half-width:\\n\\n| ŝ_run (pooled endpoint) | k=3 | k=4 | k=5 | k=7 |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 0.8 pt | 2.49 | 1.77 | **1.49** | 1.24 |\\n| 1.2 pt | 3.48 | 2.41 | **1.99** | 1.61 |\\n| 2.0 pt | 5.47 | 3.68 | **2.98** | 2.35 |\\n\\n**Second, harder limit (T5):** the *item-level* term is fixed by test-set size and observed arm discordance and is **not reduced by more seeds**: half-width 0.68 pt at p_d=0.05, 0.96 pt at 0.10, 1.36 pt at 0.20, 1.66 pt at 0.30. **Consequence, stated up front:** on the full GSM8K+MATH pair, a 0.5 pt MRD is unreachable unless p_d is small (~≤8 %) *and* ŝ_run ≲0.8 pt. Pre-registered handling: p_d and ŝ_run are estimated **only** from Tier 0 (8B) and the frozen base vs LoRA disagreement pattern, locked before 70B outcomes are unblinded; the report states the *achieved resolution* and labels the verdict at that resolution. The MRD is never widened post hoc to manufacture significance.\\n\\n**Seed count decision rule.** k = 5 preferred (gate G-B below); k = 3 permitted only as the minimum-viable tier and then reported as: \\\"the study is powered to detect ~3–5 pt differences; a null is not evidence of equivalence.\\\" One **arm-blind** variance-based seed addition is permitted if Tier 1 gives ŝ_run > 1.5 pt.\\n\\n**Decision rule.**\\n1. **Claim \\\"BAdam better on zero-shot math\\\"** only if (a) 95 % CI lower bound of Δ̂ > MRD (0.5 pt, or the locked achieved resolution if larger), **and** (b) direction consistent in ≥ ⌈k·2/3⌉+ seeds (all 3 if k=3; ≥4 of 5), **and** (c) **H2 corroboration:** the update-matched arm shows Δ_um ≥ 0 (CI upper bound above −1 pt), i.e. the parameter-matched result is *reproduced or mechanistically explained*.\\n2. **Mandatory interpretation (in the template):** a parameter-matched deficit with update-matched parity is **evidence of undertraining from the reduced per-step update budget, not of a harmful sparsity mechanism**, and must not be written as \\\"BAdam is worse\\\".\\n3. **Equivalence:** CI inside ±resolution ⇒ \\\"undetermined at this resolution\\\", never \\\"LoRA is better\\\".\\n4. Cost is descriptive: a memory/throughput win is reported in quadrant form and never as \\\"better performance\\\".\\n5. Holm correction across the 2 primary benchmarks × 2 regimes; exploratory benchmarks labelled exploratory.\\n\\n**Quadrant scheme:** accuracy-win + cost-win / accuracy-win + cost-neutral-or-worse / accuracy-null + cost-win / accuracy-null + cost-neutral, each with a pre-written sentence (§9).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Feasibility — tiered, with gates and scaling arithmetic\\n\\nBecause no GPU class, node count, or GPU-hour envelope is available to me (E3/E4: no exposed GPU, no DNS; T2 arithmetic uses *declared, unconfirmed* placeholders that must be replaced by Tier-0 measurements), the design is tiered with stop/go gates rather than budgeted.\\n\\n**Scaling formulas.** Train: `node-h ≈ 4·P·T_tok / (N_gpu·peak·MFU_train·3600)`. Eval: `node-h ≈ 2·P·T_gen / (N_gpu·peak·MFU_decode·3600)`, cross-checked against generated-token volume / (aggregate tok/s).\\n\\n**Tier 0 — 8B plumbing + fidelity (no 70B spend).** Llama-3-8B, identical recipe, all code paths, G1–G4, scorer unit test, first p_d and ŝ_run estimates, LR smoke test, memory-scaling sanity. Analytic cost T1g: ~2.8 node-hours of training + ~0.2 of eval on an 8×H100-class node.\\n- **Gate G-A (fidelity):** G1–G4 pass → proceed. Fail on G3 → proceed **accuracy-only** with a mechanism-fidelity-failure label and no cost claims; fail on G4 → halt, re-pin implementation.\\n- **Gate G-B (scorer):** ≥95 % agreement with 200 hand labels → eval harness frozen. Fail → fix scorer, do not score anything.\\n\\n**Tier 1 — minimum-viable 70B.** Arms A + B-md only, headline LR, single ρ, **k=2 seeds** for variance/memory estimation (not for a claim). Analytic cost: 4.1 h per 70B epoch on 8×H100-class at MFU 0.40; ~15 h on A100-class at MFU 0.35 (T1).\\n- **Gate G-C:** runs complete without divergence and ŝ_run is estimated → **unlocks the seed count k ∈ {3,5,7}** via §7, and the choice of ρ_um for the update-matched arm.\\n- **Gate G-D (power reality check):** locked achieved resolution from Tier 0/1 is compared against the MRD; if resolution > 1.5 pt, the study is flagged *from the outset* as unable to certify a 0.5 pt difference, and this is written into the report before outcomes.\\n\\n**Tier 2 — full design.** k seeds; parameter-matched arms; update-matched arm B-um; ρ ∈ {1/16,1/8,1/4} curve; r=64 sensitivity; LR grid; few-shot sensitivity; exploratory battery.\\n\\n**Cost matrix (analytic estimates, NOT measurements; MFU and decode-efficiency are assumptions).**\\n\\n| Configuration | Training node-h | Eval node-h | Eval share |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 8×H100-class, k=3, 1 epoch, 2 arms | 24.8 | 3.9 (MFU_dec 0.25) … 9.8 (0.10) | 14–28 % |\\n| 8×H100-class, k=5 | 41.3 | 6.2 … 15.5 | 13–27 % |\\n| 8×A100-class, k=3 | 89.7 | 12.4 … 31.0 | 12–26 % |\\n| 8×A100-class, k=5 | 149.6 | 19.6 … 49.0 | 12–25 % |\\n| Tier 0 (8B) | ~2.8 | ~0.2 | ~7 % |\\n\\n**Eval-serving is a first-class line item** (T3): the zero-shot battery generates ~9.5 M tokens per model (22.4 M with the 4/8-shot sensitivity), ≈0.16 B generated tokens across 7 models (2 arms × 3 seeds + base); that is 0.59 of one 70B training epoch in FLOPs, and 1.4–12 node-hours depending on serving efficiency — i.e. **decode-bound serving can consume a quarter to a third of the total budget**, and it is scheduled and budgeted explicitly rather than discovered late. Note the serving constraint that 70B bf16 weights are ~140 GB, so eval needs a multi-GPU replica regardless of arm.\\n**Update-matched cost law (T6):** `C_match ≈ C_LoRA · (1/ρ) · τ`, τ = BAdam step time / LoRA step time. At ρ=1/8 with τ ≥ 0.5 this is ≥4× LoRA — which is why ρ_um = 1/2 is pre-registered. This arm doubles as the honest compute-matched comparison and directly tests the efficiency claim.\\n**Storage:** base weights 140 GB/run-node; r=16 adapter 417 MB (bf16), +2.5 GB for fp32 master + moments; r=64 optimizer moments 6.63 GB all-active vs 0.83 GB at 1/8 active (T7) — the reason any large memory win must be attributed to activations/backprop, and hence why G3 is a gate.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Results / reporting template (null and memory-only outcomes are reportable by construction)\\n\\n```\\nTable 1  Accuracy (primary): arm × seed × {GSM8K, MATH} accuracy, extraction-failure %,\\n         format-failure %, Δ-from-base; full and decontaminated populations labelled.\\nTable 2  Co-primary secondary: parameter-matched vs update-matched Δ̂ with 95 % CI.\\nTable 3  Cost (descriptive): peak memory by component, tokens/s, step time, wall-clock,\\n         GPU-h, checkpoint bytes, IO volume, EVAL-SERVING node-hours.\\nTable 4  Mechanism fidelity: G1 active fraction (target vs measured), G2 Adam-state\\n         allocation, G3 activation-memory/backprop-node scaling vs ρ, G4 LoRA-equivalence.\\nTable 5  Contamination: overlap rate per benchmark, retained n, population decision taken.\\nTable 6  ρ-Pareto: (ρ, accuracy, peak memory, tokens/s) — the methodological artifact.\\nFigure 1 Loss/accuracy-vs-step curves, both arms, all seeds. Figure 2 Pareto frontier.\\n```\\nPre-written verdict sentences:\\n- **Q1 accuracy-win + cost-win:** \\\"BAdam improved zero-shot math accuracy by Δ̂ = ___ pt (95 % CI ___, ___), exceeding the pre-registered ___ pt threshold, corroborated in the update-matched regime (Δ_um = ___). It also used ___ % less peak memory at equal parameter budget.\\\"\\n- **Q2 accuracy-win + cost-neutral/worse:** \\\"…accuracy improved by Δ̂ = ___ (CI ___), corroborated/uncorroborated by the update-matched arm; the memory/throughput advantage did not materialise (Δmem = ___ %, Δtokens/s = ___ %), so BAdam's cost rationale is not supported here.\\\"\\n- **Q3 accuracy-null + cost-win:** \\\"No accuracy difference was detectable at the achieved resolution of ___ pt (Δ̂ = ___, CI ___). BAdam used ___ % less peak memory. **Cost reduction was observed; better performance was not.**\\\"\\n- **Q4 accuracy-null + cost-neutral:** \\\"Neither hypothesis was supported at the achieved resolution; the arms are statistically indistinguishable and comparable in cost.\\\"\\n- **Undertraining pattern (mandatory wording when it occurs):** \\\"Parameter-matched BAdam trails LoRA by ___ pt while the update-matched arm reaches parity (Δ_um = ___). This is evidence that the deficit follows from the reduced number of active-parameter updates, not from a harmful sparsity mechanism; it is not evidence that BAdam is worse.\\\"\\n- **Fidelity-failure:** \\\"Instrumentation showed backward-graph truncation was not realised (Table 4). The memory/throughput hypothesis could not be tested; only a sparsity-of-updates accuracy comparison is reported.\\\"\\n- **Contamination fallback:** \\\"Overlap removal would have retained only ___ % of items, below the pre-registered 80 % floor; the primary population is therefore the full test set with overlap rate ___ % disclosed.\\\"\\n- **Seed insufficiency:** \\\"With k = ___ and ŝ_run = ___ pt, this study can resolve differences of ≈___ pt; a null is a statement about resolution, not equivalence.\\\"\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Assumption A-list, with breakage mode\\n\\n| # | Assumption (NOT an established fact) | How this design breaks if wrong |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| A1 | Llama 3 70B **base** checkpoint is the starting point | With Instruct, absolute accuracies and prompt/format handling change; the arm-vs-arm contrast is likely preserved but the base-reference floor and extraction profile are invalid. |\\n| A2 | \\\"Zero-shot\\\" = 0 exemplars, greedy, no CoT trigger, no tools/self-consistency | A different convention (0-shot-CoT, or report-style prompts) changes the metric; conclusions may not transfer to those conventions. |\\n| A3 | BAdam = low-rank update + blocked BCD, inactive blocks **excluded from backprop** (Sun et al., ICLR 2024). **Unverified recollection:** the public BAdam repository reportedly had an early correctness issue that was fixed after release — *unverified, to be checked against the source, and never used as a finding.* | If backprop is not truncated, the memory/throughput hypothesis is untestable → Gate G3 fails, cost section void. If the pinned implementation differs from the paper, G4 fails → comparison invalid until re-pinned. This is precisely why the fidelity probe is a gate. |\\n| A4 | Parameter-matched is primary; update-matched corroborates | If the community norm is compute-matched primary, primary/secondary labels invert and the H1 claim loses standing. |\\n| A5 | MathInstruct = full ~240k pairs, 1 epoch, no resampling | Fewer tokens/epoch scales all costs down and may make both arms undertrained, weakening any null conclusion. |\\n| A6 | Only linear-projection adapters; no IA³/prefix/full-finetune arm | A reviewer wanting a stronger adapter baseline (e.g. DoRA/QLoRA) can argue the baseline is weak; no data exist here to answer that. |\\n| A7 | GSM8K+MATH primary; MATH500 dev-only | If a required benchmark list differs, the primary endpoint changes post hoc — a pre-registration violation. |\\n| A8 | ρ headline from paper default, else MathInstruct-dev knee | Choosing ρ on any benchmark would inflate BAdam (or LoRA) and void the comparison. |\\n| A9 | Achievable MFU 0.35–0.40 (train), decode efficiency 0.10–0.25 | All node-hour figures shift proportionally; an under-budgeted eval phase can stop Tier 2 mid-sweep. |\\n| A10 | Weights and MathInstruct are licensable/downloadable in the executing environment | Nothing runs; design remains unexecuted (correctly, since nothing has run here). |\\n| A11 | ŝ_run ≲1 pt, p_d ≲0.10–0.20 | If larger, §7's locked resolution exceeds the 0.5 pt MRD and the study can only report equivalence/undetermined — by design, not by failure. |\\n| A12 | Gradient checkpointing + sharding identical across arms | If not, memory comparisons are meaningless. |\\n| A13 | Scoring by symbolic equivalence ≈ human grading | Systematic extraction bias would distort accuracy and, worse, differentially penalise one arm's output style; mitigated by G0 (≥95 % hand-check) and per-arm extraction-failure reporting. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. External-validity note (practitioner starting from Instruct)\\nA practitioner will almost always start from `Llama-3-70B-Instruct`. There, absolute accuracies are far higher (the zero-shot floor-artefact risk largely disappears, so the few-shot sensitivity arm matters less), the input distribution is already chat-formatted so the adapter's job is narrower and lower-capacity — which plausibly *shrinks* any rank-limited accuracy gap between arms and can compress both toward the same ceiling — while the cost axis is unchanged or worsened, since serving/finetuning an instruct model typically uses longer chat templates. Two effects could flip the practical conclusion: an instruct model has fewer headroom points to gain, so a null becomes the expected outcome, and format/answer-style drift caused by finetuning can *degrade* an already-strong instruct model, making extraction-failure rate a first-order outcome rather than a diagnostic. This design's arm-vs-arm contrast should transfer qualitatively, but its absolute accuracies, its MRD appropriateness, and its null-vs-regression interpretation should not be quoted for an instruct starting point without a dedicated run.\\n\\n## 12. Citation defensibility\\nMechanism-level attribution only: LoRA (Hu et al., 2021) — frozen base weights plus trainable low-rank pairs on selected linear projections, all trainable parameters updated every step. BAdam (Sun et al., ICLR 2024) — blocked block-coordinate descent over low-rank parameters, a subset activated per step, advertised for memory-efficient finetuning of large models. I verified **no** repository, paper text, hyperparameter table, or default ρ here; every version-dependent detail (default block ratio, α convention, whether backprop is truncated, the state of the early public release) must be checked against the pinned source before Gate G-A, and is flagged as unverified in A-list A3.\\n\\n## 13. Pre-registration artifacts to freeze before any run\\nconfig bundle + hashes; ρ grid and headline-selection rule; LR grid and dev-slice selection rule; seed list; contamination thresholds and population rule; scorer version + 200-item hand-label set; analysis script with locked estimators, MRD/resolution procedure, quadrant sentences; fidelity-gate pass criteria. All frozen before 70B outcomes are unblinded.\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-807d1dbc-17ed-48bf-97bc-177d6f73b344", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": null, "kind": "ActionEvent"}
