{"id": "e3f3ee4d-3b4f-40e8-8e3f-4d2db4437a62", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:25:59.446689", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "3e39c163-4231-43c7-9cf3-5cea27287fa2", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "I need to include evidence, assumptions, uncertainties along with the answer. Let me submit the full call again with all fields.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# EXPERIMENT DESIGN — BAdam vs LoRA on Llama 3 70B + MathInstruct, zero-shot math transfer\n\n**Status / provenance.** Design-only artifact. Nothing was trained, downloaded, or measured. Every number below is either (i) a *direct sandbox probe* (E-numbered, in Evidence), or (ii) **analytic arithmetic** under explicitly declared assumptions (T-numbered). No GPU was used. Citations are limited to mechanism-level attribution (author/venue/year), with version-dependent details flagged unverified.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Question, hypotheses, endpoints\n\n**Primary question.** Does BAdam beat LoRA on zero-shot math when finetuning Llama 3 70B base on MathInstruct?\n\n- **H1 (accuracy, primary, parameter-matched):** pooled zero-shot accuracy of BAdam > LoRA.\n- **H2 (corroboration, CO-PRIMARY SECONDARY, update-volume-matched):** under equal *active-parameter-update volume*, BAdam ≥ LoRA.\n- **H3 (cost, descriptive only, never a claim of \"better performance\"):** BAdam uses less peak memory and/or more tokens/s at equal parameter budget.\n\n**Endpoints.** Primary: `A_pooled = mean(GSM8K acc, MATH acc)` per run on the pre-registered test population (§5). Co-primary secondary: same quantity in the update-matched regime. Descriptive: component-wise memory, throughput, wall-clock, storage/IO, extraction- and format-failure rates; exploratory benchmarks.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Arms and configurations\n\n| Arm | Method | Rank / modules | Active fraction per step | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A | LoRA (Hu et al., 2021) | r=16, all 7 linears/block (q,k,v,o,gate,up,down), α=32 | 1 (all params) | Primary baseline |\n| B-hi / B-md / B-lo | BAdam (Sun et al., ICLR 2024) | identical to A | ρ = 1/4, 1/8, 1/16 | Treatment, ρ grid |\n| A-lr | LoRA | r=16 | 1 | LR grid (½×, 1×, 2×) |\n| B-um | BAdam, update-matched | identical to A | ρ_um = **1/2** (largest in grid; cost reason §8) | Co-primary secondary |\n| C0 | Frozen base | — | — | Reference / floor |\n| A64, B64 | LoRA / BAdam | r=64 | 1 / ρ_md | Sensitivity |\n\n**Mechanism attributed to BAdam** (defensible level): a LoRA-style low-rank reparameterisation of the weight update on frozen base weights trained by **blocked block-coordinate descent** — low-rank parameters are partitioned into blocks and only one block (an ρ fraction of the low-rank parameters) is activated and Adam-updated per step. The claimed memory/throughput advantage additionally requires that **inactive blocks are excluded from the backward graph**; this is the design's central falsifiable engineering assumption, hence Gate G3.\n\n**ρ is a declared hyperparameter, not a constant.** Pre-registered grid ρ ∈ {1/16, 1/8, 1/4}; total steps are chosen so an integer number of complete block cycles fits in the run (coverage fairness: every low-rank parameter receives the same number of update slots by construction). Headline setting, in strict precedence: (1) the reference implementation/paper default *if verifiable against the source*; else (2) the knee of the accuracy–memory Pareto curve computed **only on a MathInstruct development slice** (2,000 held-out pairs; training loss + extractable-answer rate on held-out MathInstruct items); **never** on any benchmark. The full ρ curve, not a single point, is the methodological artifact for the cost axis.\n\n### 2.1 Mechanism-fidelity probe — HARD GATE (not a footnote)\nRun at Tier 0 before any 70B spend.\n- **G1 active-parameter census:** instrumented per-step count of parameters receiving a nonzero update; must satisfy |active|/|trainable| = ρ ± 1 %, with uniform block coverage over a cycle (χ² check).\n- **G2 optimizer-state allocation:** Adam moment buffers must exist only for active blocks (asserted on allocation records, not on reported totals).\n- **G3 backward-graph truncation (the decisive one):** at identical micro-batch, sequence length and gradient-checkpointing, compare (i) peak activation memory and (ii) autograd-node / backward-kernel counts across ρ = 1, 1/2, 1/8, 1/16. Both must scale with the number of layers holding active blocks. **If G3 fails the memory/throughput hypothesis is untestable as specified**: the study is reported as a *sparsity-of-updates* accuracy comparison with an explicit mechanism-fidelity-failure statement, and no cost claim of any kind is permitted.\n- **G4 gradient-consistency sanity:** with ρ = 1, BAdam must reproduce LoRA's loss curve within tolerance (same seed, same data order). Failure ⇒ the implementation is not a superset of LoRA and the comparison is invalid; halt and re-pin.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Controls (all pre-registered; the only intended difference is the update rule)\n\nIdentical across arms: base checkpoint hash; tokenizer/chat template; train split and per-seed shuffled order (seed-derived permutation shared across arms); sequence packing and drop-last policy; max seq len (2 048 default, 4 096 sensitivity); global effective batch in tokens; AdamW (β1=0.9, β2=0.95, wd=0, ε=1e-8); LR peak and cosine shape with 3 % warmup; epoch/step count; loss mask (prompt masked, solution supervised); no resampling/filtering; bf16 autocast, TF32 on, FlashAttention on, **identical gradient-checkpointing**; identical FSDP/ZeRO-3 sharding plan, node count, micro-batch, storage backend and page-cache state. Fairness is further protected by declaring \"best LR per arm within the declared ½×/1×/2× grid, selected on the MathInstruct dev slice only\" as the comparison rule, so neither arm is strawmanned. No early stopping; the final checkpoint is the evaluated artifact (fixed-interval checkpoints give descriptive curves only). Eval logs are hash-logged to evidence the no-peeking rule.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Data and contamination protocol (pre-registered fallback)\n\nMathInstruct (~240k pairs) is the only training data; benchmarks are read-only, checksummed, never used for LR selection, checkpoint selection, or stopping.\n\n**Detection (run before training, frozen):** normalise questions (lowercase, strip punctuation, mask numerals), then flag a test item as *leaked-adjacent* if (i) exact match to a MathInstruct question, or (ii) ≥ 0.85 masked-5-gram containment (sensitivity threshold 0.60). Report overlap rates per benchmark; these are **unknown until measured**. Note: MathInstruct is reported to derive partly from GSM8K/MATH **train** sources (recollection, to verify), so test leakage should be near-duplicate rather than split inversion.\n\n**Pre-registered population rule (the tightened item):**\n- Primary population = **decontaminated** subset **iff** retained fraction ≥ **0.80 on both** GSM8K and MATH **and** n_GSM8K ≥ 1 050 and n_MATH ≥ 4 000.\n- Otherwise the primary population **reverts to the full test set**, with the overlap rate disclosed in the headline table and the decontaminated analysis demoted to a sensitivity analysis.\n- Rationale (T5): with paired item discordance p_d, the pooled endpoint's item-level 95 % half-width is `0.5·√(p_d/n_g + p_d/n_m)`. At p_d = 0.15 the full sets already give ≈1.17 pt, so the ±0.5 pt MRD cannot absorb aggressive filtering; the 0.80 / 1 050 / 4 000 thresholds keep the item half-width ≤ ~1.5 pt even under the pessimistic p_d = 0.20.\n\n**The logic that actually protects the comparison:** both arms share the same base model and the same data, so leakage inflates *both*. The asymmetric risk is differential memorisation (a sparsely-updated model may memorise leaked items less), which biases **against BAdam**. Therefore report Δ-from-base alongside arm-vs-arm, and report the arm difference separately on leaked-adjacent vs clean items; if the arms differ only on leaked-adjacent items, interpret as memorisation, not reasoning.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Evaluation protocol\n\n**Zero-shot (primary), stated to remove ambiguity:** one fixed instruction template, **zero exemplars**, no CoT trigger beyond the neutral instruction, greedy decoding (T=0; no sampling, no self-consistency, no calculator/tool use, no answer-checker reranking), max 1 024 new tokens.\n**Few-shot (4- and 8-shot, fixed exemplar sets, identical across arms) is a floor-artefact sensitivity only** — reported, never used for the headline claim or for selection.\n\n**Primary benchmarks:** GSM8K test (1 319), MATH test (5 000). MATH500 is a development subset **only** (never for selection or headline reporting). **Exploratory:** GSM-Hard, SVAMP, MultiArith, MMLU-STEM/math, AQuA, TheoremQA.\n\n**Scoring (frozen before any arm is scored):** deterministic two-stage — (1) format-aware extraction (last `\\boxed{...}` → \"final answer is/=\" → last numeric expression); (2) mathematical-equity grading by symbolic normalisation (sympy-style), string match for MCQ. **Extraction failures are counted incorrect** and reported as a per-arm rate beside accuracy, because differential format breakdown is itself an outcome. The scorer is frozen, versioned, and unit-tested against 200 hand-labelled generations (Gate G-B requires ≥ 95 % agreement) before any arm is scored.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Cost metrics (descriptive, first-class)\n\nPer arm, per seed, instrumented: peak per-GPU memory decomposed into **weights / activations / gradients / optimizer state**; steady-state tokens/s (excluding first 5 % of steps); step time; wall-clock to the accuracy endpoint; GPU-hours; checkpoint bytes (adapter vs optimizer state); IO volume and dataloader wait fraction; **eval-serving cost as its own line item** (§8). Memory deltas are reported *only with* component attribution — an unattributed memory delta is not reported. Gradient checkpointing is a declared constant, not a free variable, because it dominates peak memory.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Statistical analysis plan and honest power\n\n**Estimator (primary, pre-registered):** seed-level paired analysis — Δ_s = A_pooled(BAdam,s) − A_pooled(LoRA,s) over matched seeds; mean Δ̂ with a t-based 95 % CI on k−1 df, plus a mixed-effects logistic model on item-level correctness (fixed: arm, benchmark, regime; random: seed, item) as model-based confirmation. **Item-level bootstrap CIs are secondary and explicitly labelled as understating uncertainty**, since they treat training as deterministic; McNemar/permutation tests are descriptive only.\n\n**Seed-count honesty (T4; no claim that k=3 suffices).** Seed-term CI half-width = `t_{k−1}·ŝ_run/√k`; a superiority claim over MRD = 0.5 pt needs Δ̂ > 0.5 + that half-width:\n\n| ŝ_run (pooled endpoint) | k=3 | k=4 | k=5 | k=7 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 0.8 pt | 2.49 | 1.77 | **1.49** | 1.24 |\n| 1.2 pt | 3.48 | 2.41 | **1.99** | 1.61 |\n| 2.0 pt | 5.47 | 3.68 | **2.98** | 2.35 |\n\n**Second, harder limit (T5):** the *item-level* term is fixed by test-set size and observed discordance and is **not reduced by more seeds**: 95 % half-width 0.68 pt at p_d=0.05, 0.96 at 0.10, 1.36 at 0.20, 1.66 at 0.30. **Consequence stated up front:** on the full GSM8K+MATH pair a 0.5 pt MRD is unreachable unless p_d ≲8 % *and* ŝ_run ≲0.8 pt. Handling: p_d and ŝ_run are estimated **only** from Tier 0 (8B) and the frozen-base-vs-LoRA disagreement pattern, locked before 70B outcomes are unblinded; the report states the *achieved resolution* and labels the verdict at that resolution. The MRD is never widened post hoc to manufacture significance.\n\n**Seed-count decision rule.** k = 5 preferred (gate G-C); k = 3 permitted only as minimum-viable and then reported as \"powered to resolve ~3–5 pt; a null is not evidence of equivalence\". One **arm-blind, variance-based** seed addition is permitted if Tier 1 yields ŝ_run > 1.5 pt.\n\n**Decision rule.**\n1. Claim \"BAdam better on zero-shot math\" only if (a) the 95 % CI lower bound of Δ̂ exceeds MRD (0.5 pt, or the locked achieved resolution if larger), **and** (b) direction is consistent in ≥ ⌈2k/3⌉+1... i.e. all 3 if k=3 and ≥4 of 5, **and** (c) **H2 corroboration:** the update-matched arm shows Δ_um ≥ 0 (CI upper bound above −1 pt), i.e. the parameter-matched result is *reproduced or mechanistically explained*.\n2. **Mandatory interpretation:** a parameter-matched deficit with update-matched parity is **evidence of undertraining from the reduced per-step update budget, not of a harmful sparsity mechanism**, and must not be written as \"BAdam is worse\".\n3. Equivalence band: CI inside ±resolution ⇒ \"undetermined at this resolution\", never \"LoRA is better\".\n4. Cost is descriptive; a memory/throughput win is reported in quadrant form and never as \"better performance\".\n5. Holm correction across 2 primary benchmarks × 2 regimes; exploratory benchmarks labelled exploratory.\n\n**Quadrant scheme:** accuracy-win+cost-win / accuracy-win+cost-neutral-or-worse / accuracy-null+cost-win / accuracy-null+cost-neutral, each with a pre-written sentence (§9).\n\n---\n\n## 8. Feasibility — tiered, with gates and scaling arithmetic\n\nNo GPU class, node count or GPU-hour envelope is available to me (E3/E4: no exposed GPU, no DNS), so the T-numbers use *declared, unconfirmed* placeholders that **must be replaced by Tier-0 measured values**; nothing here assumes a specific cluster.\n\n**Scaling formulas.** Train: `node-h ≈ 4·P·T_tok / (N_gpu·peak·MFU_train·3600)`. Eval: `node-h ≈ 2·P·T_gen / (N_gpu·peak·MFU_decode·3600)`, cross-checked against generated-token volume ÷ aggregate tokens/s.\n\n**Tier 0 — 8B plumbing + fidelity (no 70B spend).** Llama-3-8B, identical recipe, all code paths, G1–G4, scorer unit test, first p_d and ŝ_run estimates, LR smoke test, memory-scaling sanity. Analytic cost ~2.8 node-hours training + ~0.2 eval on an 8×H100-class node.\n- **Gate G-A (fidelity):** G1–G4 pass → proceed. Fail on G3 → proceed **accuracy-only** with a mechanism-fidelity-failure label and no cost claims. Fail on G4 → halt, re-pin implementation.\n- **Gate G-B (scorer):** ≥95 % agreement with 200 hand labels → freeze eval harness. Fail → fix scorer; score nothing.\n\n**Tier 1 — minimum-viable 70B.** Arms A + B-md only, headline LR, single ρ, **k=2 seeds** for variance/memory estimation (not for claims). Analytic: ~4.1 h per 70B epoch on 8×H100-class at MFU 0.40; ~15 h on A100-class at MFU 0.35.\n- **Gate G-C:** runs complete without divergence and ŝ_run is estimated → **unlocks k ∈ {3,5,7}** via §7 and the ρ_um choice.\n- **Gate G-D (power reality check):** compare locked achieved resolution with the MRD; if resolution > 1.5 pt the study is flagged *before unblinding* as unable to certify a 0.5 pt difference, and this is written into the report.\n\n**Tier 2 — full design.** k seeds; parameter-matched arms; update-matched arm B-um; ρ ∈ {1/16,1/8,1/4} curve; r=64 sensitivity; LR grid; few-shot sensitivity; exploratory battery.\n\n**Cost matrix (analytic estimates, NOT measurements; MFU and decode efficiency are assumptions).**\n\n| Configuration | Training node-h | Eval node-h | Eval share |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 8×H100-class, k=3, 1 epoch, 2 arms | 24.8 | 3.9 (MFU_dec 0.25) … 9.8 (0.10) | 14–28 % |\n| 8×H100-class, k=5 | 41.3 | 6.2 … 15.5 | 13–27 % |\n| 8×A100-class, k=3 | 89.7 | 12.4 … 31.0 | 12–26 % |\n| 8×A100-class, k=5 | 149.6 | 19.6 … 49.0 | 12–25 % |\n| Tier 0 (8B) | ~2.8 | ~0.2 | ~7 % |\n\n**Eval-serving is a first-class line item (T3):** the zero-shot battery generates ≈9.5 M tokens per model (22.4 M with the 4/8-shot sensitivity) — ≈0.16 B generated tokens across 7 models (2 arms × 3 seeds + base), i.e. 0.59 of one 70B training epoch in FLOPs and 1.4–12 node-hours depending on serving efficiency. **Decode-bound serving can therefore consume a quarter to a third of the total budget**, and is budgeted explicitly rather than discovered late; note that 70B bf16 weights (~140 GB) force a multi-GPU serving replica for evaluation regardless of arm.\n**Update-matched cost law (T6):** `C_match ≈ C_LoRA · (1/ρ) · τ`, τ = BAdam step time / LoRA step time. At ρ = 1/8 with τ ≥ 0.5 this is ≥4× LoRA, which is why ρ_um = 1/2 is pre-registered; this arm doubles as the honest compute-matched comparison and directly tests the efficiency claim.\n**Storage:** base weights 140 GB per run; r=16 adapter 417 MB bf16 plus ~2.5 GB fp32 master + moments; r=64 optimizer moments 6.63 GB all-active vs 0.83 GB at 1/8 active (T7) — the reason any large memory win must be attributed to activations/backprop, and hence why G3 is a gate.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Results / reporting template (null and memory-only outcomes reportable by construction)\n\n```\nT1 Accuracy (primary): arm × seed × {GSM8K, MATH} accuracy, extraction-failure %,\n   format-failure %, Δ-from-base; full and decontaminated populations labelled.\nT2 Co-primary secondary: parameter-matched vs update-matched Δ̂ with 95 % CI.\nT3 Cost (descriptive): peak memory by component, tokens/s, step time, wall-clock,\n   GPU-h, checkpoint bytes, IO volume, EVAL-SERVING node-hours.\nT4 Mechanism fidelity: G1 active fraction (target vs measured), G2 Adam-state\n   allocation, G3 activation-memory/backprop-node scaling vs ρ, G4 LoRA-equivalence.\nT5 Contamination: overlap rate per benchmark, retained n, population decision taken.\nT6 ρ-Pareto: (ρ, accuracy, peak memory, tokens/s) — the methodological artifact.\nF1 Loss/accuracy-vs-step curves, both arms, all seeds.  F2 Pareto frontier.\n```\nPre-written verdict sentences:\n- **Q1 accuracy-win + cost-win:** \"BAdam improved zero-shot math accuracy by Δ̂ = ___ pt (95 % CI ___, ___), exceeding the pre-registered ___ pt threshold, corroborated in the update-matched regime (Δ_um = ___). It also used ___ % less peak memory at equal parameter budget.\"\n- **Q2 accuracy-win + cost-neutral/worse:** \"Accuracy improved by Δ̂ = ___ (CI ___), corroborated/uncorroborated by the update-matched arm; the memory/throughput advantage did not materialise (Δmem = ___ %, Δtokens/s = ___ %), so BAdam's cost rationale is not supported here.\"\n- **Q3 accuracy-null + cost-win:** \"No accuracy difference was detectable at the achieved resolution of ___ pt (Δ̂ = ___, CI ___). BAdam used ___ % less peak memory. **Cost reduction was observed; better performance was not.**\"\n- **Q4 accuracy-null + cost-neutral:** \"Neither hypothesis was supported at the achieved resolution; the arms are statistically indistinguishable and comparable in cost.\"\n- **Undertraining pattern (mandatory wording when it occurs):** \"Parameter-matched BAdam trails LoRA by ___ pt while the update-matched arm reaches parity (Δ_um = ___). This is evidence that the deficit follows from the reduced number of active-parameter updates, not from a harmful sparsity mechanism; it is not evidence that BAdam is worse.\"\n- **Fidelity failure:** \"Instrumentation showed backward-graph truncation was not realised (T4). The memory/throughput hypothesis could not be tested; only a sparsity-of-updates accuracy comparison is reported.\"\n- **Contamination fallback:** \"Overlap removal would have retained only ___ % of items, below the pre-registered 80 % floor; the primary population is therefore the full test set with overlap rate ___ % disclosed.\"\n- **Seed insufficiency:** \"With k = ___ and ŝ_run = ___ pt this study resolves differences of ≈___ pt; a null is a statement about resolution, not equivalence.\"\n\n---\n\n## 10. Assumption A-list, with breakage mode\n\n| # | Assumption (NOT an established fact) | How this design breaks if wrong |\n|---|---|---|\n| A1 | Llama 3 70B **base** is the starting point | With Instruct, absolute accuracies and prompt/format handling change; the arm-vs-arm contrast likely transfers but the base-reference floor and extraction profile do not. |\n| A2 | \"Zero-shot\" = 0 exemplars, greedy, no CoT trigger, no tools/self-consistency | A different convention (0-shot-CoT, report-style prompts) changes the metric; conclusions may not transfer. |\n| A3 | BAdam = low-rank update + blocked BCD with inactive blocks **excluded from backprop** (Sun et al., ICLR 2024). **Unverified recollection:** the public BAdam repository reportedly had an early correctness issue fixed after release — *unverified, to be checked against the pinned source, never used as a finding.* | If backprop is not truncated, the memory/throughput hypothesis is untestable → G3 fails, cost section void. If the pinned implementation differs from the paper, G4 fails → comparison invalid until re-pinned. This is exactly why the fidelity probe is a gate. |\n| A4 | Parameter-matched primary; update-matched corroborates | If the community norm is compute-matched primary, the primary/secondary labels invert and the H1 claim loses standing. |\n| A5 | MathInstruct = full ~240k pairs, 1 epoch, no resampling | Fewer tokens/epoch scales all costs down but may leave both arms undertrained, weakening any null conclusion. |\n| A6 | Only linear-projection adapters; no IA³/prefix/DoRA/full-finetune arm | A reviewer can argue the baseline is weak relative to stronger adapters; no data here answer that. |\n| A7 | GSM8K+MATH primary; MATH500 dev-only | A different required benchmark list changes the endpoint post hoc — a pre-registration violation. |\n| A8 | ρ headline from paper default, else MathInstruct-dev knee | Choosing ρ using any benchmark would inflate one arm and void the comparison. |\n| A9 | MFU 0.35–0.40 training, decode efficiency 0.10–0.25 | All node-hour figures shift proportionally; an under-budgeted eval phase can stop Tier 2 mid-sweep. |\n| A10 | Weights and MathInstruct are obtainable/licensable in the executing environment | Nothing runs; the design stays unexecuted (correctly — nothing has run here). |\n| A11 | ŝ_run ≲1 pt, p_d ≲0.10–0.20 | If larger, the locked resolution exceeds the 0.5 pt MRD and the study can only report equivalence/undetermined — by design, not failure. |\n| A12 | Gradient checkpointing and sharding identical across arms | Otherwise memory comparisons are meaningless. |\n| A13 | Symbolic-equivalence scoring ≈ human grading | Systematic extraction bias would distort accuracy and differentially penalise one arm's output style; mitigated by Gate G-B and per-arm extraction-failure reporting. |\n\n---\n\n## 11. External-validity note (practitioner starting from Instruct)\nA practitioner will almost always start from `Llama-3-70B-Instruct`. There, absolute accuracies are far higher, so the zero-shot floor-artefact risk largely disappears and the few-shot sensitivity arm matters less; the input distribution is already chat-formatted, so the adapter's job is narrower and lower-capacity, which plausibly **shrinks** any capacity-limited gap between arms and compresses both toward a ceiling — while the cost axis is unchanged or worse, since chat templates are longer. Two effects could flip the practical conclusion: an instruct model has fewer headroom points, making a null the expected outcome, and finetuning can *degrade* an already-strong instruct model, promoting extraction/format-failure rate from diagnostic to first-order outcome. This design's arm-vs-arm contrast should transfer qualitatively, but its absolute accuracies, the appropriateness of its 0.5 pt MRD, and its null-vs-regression reading should not be quoted for an instruct starting point without a dedicated run.\n\n## 12. Citation defensibility\nMechanism-level attribution only: **LoRA** (Hu et al., 2021) — frozen base weights plus trainable low-rank pairs on selected linear projections, all trainable parameters updated every step. **BAdam** (Sun et al., ICLR 2024) — blocked block-coordinate descent over low-rank parameters, a subset activated per step, advertised for memory-efficient finetuning of large models. I verified **no** repository, paper text, hyperparameter table, or default ρ in this environment; every version-dependent detail (default block ratio, α convention, whether backprop is truncated, the state of the early public release) must be checked against the pinned source before Gate G-A and is flagged unverified in A3.\n\n## 13. Pre-registration artifacts to freeze before any run\nConfig bundle + hashes; ρ grid and headline-selection rule; LR grid and dev-slice selection rule; seed list; contamination thresholds and population rule; scorer version + 200-item hand-label set; analysis script with locked estimators, MRD/resolution procedure and quadrant sentences; fidelity-gate pass criteria — all frozen before 70B outcomes are unblinded.", "evidence": "**E — direct sandbox probes (exploratory environment checks only; no experiment, no training, no download)**\n\n- **E1 Workspace is empty of research inputs.** `ls -la /workspace` and `find /workspace -maxdepth 3` → single file `task.json` (613 B). `cat /workspace/task.json` contains only the question, \"Do not claim that an experiment was run\", and `documents.track.md: \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"` No repository, no source code, no data.\n- **E2 No ML stack, no datasets, no reference implementations.** `python3 -V` → 3.14.4. `import torch`, `import transformers`, `import peft` → all `ModuleNotFoundError`. `pip list` shows only system packages. `find / -maxdepth 3` for `*mathinstruct*`, `*llama*3*`, `*badam*`, `*lora*` → no matches. No `~/.cache` and no `~/.cache/huggingface`. **MathInstruct, Llama 3-70B weights, and any BAdam/LoRA reference implementation are NOT locally available; nothing was downloaded or run.**\n- **E3 No usable GPU.** `/usr/bin/nvidia-smi` exists but prints \"NVIDIA-SMI has failed because it couldn't communicate with the NVIDIA driver\"; `ls /dev/nvidia*` → no device nodes (only `/dev/nvidia-caps`); `lspci` lists host GB100-class 3D controllers that are **not exposed** into this bubblewrap sandbox; task spec states `gpu_enabled: false`.\n- **E4 No network.** `socket.create_connection(('pypi.org',443))` → `socket.gaierror: Temporary failure in name resolution`. Consistent with `network: disabled`; no install or download possible. (Python 3.14 is a secondary practical blocker for CUDA wheels.)\n- **E5 Irrelevant host resources.** `nproc` = 224; `free -g` ≈ 2011 GB RAM; `/` and `/tmp` are tmpfs ~1006 GB with 0 used. CPU/RAM cannot substitute for the accelerator training requires.\n\n**T — analytic arithmetic (formulas + inputs stated; these are estimates, NOT measurements)**\n\n- **T1 Training volume.** 240 000 samples × ~700 tokens = 1.68×10⁸ tokens/epoch; 4·P·T with P=70×10⁹ → **4.704×10¹⁹ FLOPs per 70B epoch** → ~4.1 h on an 8×H100-class node (peak 989 TFLOP/s/GPU, MFU 0.40) and ~15.0 h on an 8×A100-80G-class node (312 TFLOP/s, MFU 0.35). k seeds × 2 arms: k=3 → 24.8 node-h (H100-class) / 89.7 (A100-class); k=5 → 41.3 / 149.6.\n- **T2 Decode/eval model.** Generation FLOPs = 2·P·(prompt+output tokens) per item; zero-shot battery (MATH 5000, GSM8K 1319, GSM-Hard 1319, SVAMP 1000, MultiArith 600, MMLU-math 1800, AQuA 254, TheoremQA 800) = 1.757×10¹⁸ FLOPs per model; adding 4- and 8-shot on the two primaries = 3.993×10¹⁸ per model; ×7 models (2 arms × 3 seeds + frozen base) = 2.795×10¹⁹.\n- **T3 Eval-serving as a first-class cost.** = 0.59 of one 70B training epoch in FLOPs. Token-volume cross-check: 9.49 M generated tokens per model zero-shot, 22.43 M with the few-shot sensitivity, 0.157 B across 7 models → 1.4–5.5 node-hours at 4000–1000 aggregate tokens/s/GPU on 8 GPUs; FLOPs-based with MFU_dec 0.25 → 3.9 (H100-class) / 12.4 (A100-class), and 9.8 / 31.0 at MFU_dec 0.10. Eval share of the total budget: 12–28 %. Raising max_new_tokens 1024→2048 multiplies long-output sets by ~1.67. Serving needs ≥140 GB of bf16 weights → multi-GPU replica irrespective of arm.\n- **T4 Seed sizing (used in §7 table).** Seed-term half-width = t_{k−1}·ŝ_run/√k (t₍df2₎=4.303, df3=3.182, df4=2.776, df6=2.447, df8=2.365). For ŝ_run=0.8/1.2/2.0 pt and k=3/4/5/7/9 the smallest claimable Δ̂ over a 0.5 pt MRD ranges 1.13→5.47 pt; e.g. ŝ_run=1.2: k=3 → 3.48 pt, k=5 → 1.99 pt, k=7 → 1.61 pt.\n- **T5 Item-level floor and contamination power.** Paired item SE on the pooled endpoint = 0.5·√(p_d/n_g + p_d/n_m) with n_g=1319, n_m=5000 → half-width 0.68 pt (p_d=0.05), 0.96 (0.10), 1.17 (0.15), 1.36 (0.20), 1.66 (0.30). Filtering both sets to retained fraction f: at p_d=0.20, f=0.8 → 1.52 pt, f=0.6 → 1.75, f=0.4 → 2.15; at p_d=0.10, f=0.8 → 1.07, f=0.6 → 1.24, f=0.4 → 1.52. This is the arithmetic basis for the pre-registered ≥0.80 retention / n_GSM8K ≥ 1050 / n_MATH ≥ 4000 floor (≤~1.5 pt half-width even at p_d=0.20) and the revert-to-full-set fallback.\n- **T6 Update-matching cost law.** C_match ≈ C_LoRA · (1/ρ) · τ with τ = BAdam step time / LoRA step time; at ρ=1/8, τ≥0.5 ⇒ ≥4× LoRA cost — the stated reason ρ_um = 1/2 is pre-registered for the co-primary secondary arm.\n- **T7 Parameter/memory arithmetic from Llama-3-70B geometry** (80 layers, d=8192, kv-head total dim 1024, ffn 28672, 7 target linears/block; per-block Σ(in+out)=162 832): r=8 → 103.5 M trainable (0.148 %), r=16 → 207.1 M (0.296 %), r=32 → 414.2 M (0.592 %), r=64 → 828.4 M (1.183 %) of 70 B. All-active fp32 Adam moments at r=64 ≈ 6.63 GB vs ≈0.83 GB at 1/8 active fraction; bf16 70B weights = 140 GB; r=16 adapter = 417 MB bf16, +2.5 GB fp32 master + moments. Because optimizer-state savings are single-digit GB, any large memory win must come from activation/backprop truncation — which is why G3 is a hard gate rather than a footnote.\n- **T8 Tier-0 pilot sizing.** 8B training scales by P: 3 seeds × 2 arms × 1 epoch ≈ 2.83 node-h and ≈0.2 node-h eval on an 8×H100-class node (same formulas as T1/T2 with P=8×10⁹); 70B/8B compute ratio ≈ 8.75×.\n\n**Repository observation.** There is no repository to integrate with: the entire task surface is a single 613-byte JSON containing the question and a design-only note. Consequently the design specifies its own toolchain requirements (pinned BAdam and PEFT implementations, FSDP/ZeRO-3 trainer, vLLM/HF-serving-style batched greedy decoding for eval) as artifacts to be created and hash-frozen at Tier 0, rather than modifications to existing code — and every implementation-dependent quantity (default ρ, α convention, backprop truncation) is deferred to the pinned-source verification step instead of being asserted.", "assumptions": ["A1 The finetuning starting point is the Llama 3 70B **base** checkpoint (design directive); with an Instruct start, absolute accuracies, prompt handling and the null-vs-regression reading change (§11).", "A2 'Zero-shot' is fixed as: zero in-context exemplars, one neutral instruction, greedy decoding, no CoT trigger, no tools, no self-consistency, no answer-checker reranking.", "A3 BAdam is attributed a LoRA-style low-rank update trained by blocked block-coordinate descent with an ρ fraction of low-rank parameters active per step, and — the load-bearing engineering assumption — inactive blocks excluded from the backward graph. The recollection of an early post-release correctness fix in the public BAdam repo is explicitly UNVERIFIED and is to be checked against the pinned source; it is tied to gates G3/G4 and is never used as a finding.", "A4 Parameter-matched is the primary comparison; update-volume-matched is the co-primary secondary that must corroborate or explain the primary.", "A5 MathInstruct is used as ~240k pairs for 1 epoch with no resampling or filtering; the token-volume arithmetic scales linearly if this changes.", "A6 Only linear-projection adapters are compared; no IA3/prefix/DoRA/full-finetune arm (full finetuning of 70B is assumed out of budget).", "A7 GSM8K test + MATH test are the primary zero-shot endpoints, MATH500 is development-only, and GSM-Hard/SVAMP/MultiArith/MMLU-math/AQuA/TheoremQA are exploratory.", "A8 No GPU class, node count, MFU or GPU-hour envelope is assumed: all cost figures are parameterised by (GPU class x count x MFU) placeholders to be replaced by Tier-0 measurements.", "A9 Training and decode MFU placeholders are 0.35-0.40 and 0.10-0.25 respectively; average sample length ~700 tokens; average prompt ~200-350 tokens with 1024-token generation caps.", "A10 Llama 3-70B weights and MathInstruct are obtainable and licensable in some hypothetical executing environment; nothing is available or downloaded here.", "A11 Achievable statistical resolution is treated as an empirical quantity estimated only from Tier 0/Tier 1 (arm-blind) and locked before 70B outcomes are unblinded; the 0.5 pt MRD is never widened post hoc.", "A12 Gradient checkpointing, sharding plan, batch size in tokens and sequence length are identical across arms, so peak-memory comparisons are interpretable.", "A13 Symbolic-equivalence grading approximates human judgement; extraction failures count as incorrect and are reported per arm.", "A14 Mechanism-level attributions to Hu et al. 2021 and Sun et al. ICLR 2024 are at the level defensible from general knowledge; no paper text, repository, or hyperparameter table was inspected here."], "uncertainties": ["Statistical resolution, not effort, is the binding constraint: with 1319+5000 test items the paired item-level term alone gives a ~1.0-1.7 pt half-width, so a 0.5 pt MRD may be unclaimable at ANY seed count unless observed arm discordance is low. This is disclosed pre-unblinding rather than resolved by more compute.", "The true run-level SD (s_run) of the pooled endpoint is unknown; if it exceeds ~1.5 pt the pre-registered arm-blind seed addition triggers and Tier 2 cost rises ~40-60%.", "Whether the pinned BAdam implementation actually truncates backprop for inactive blocks (Gate G3). If not, the entire cost hypothesis is untestable and the study silently becomes an accuracy-only sparsity comparison.", "The default per-step active-block ratio in the reference implementation, and whether the paper's reported configuration is reproducible; this determines the headline rho and therefore the headline result.", "The unverified recollection of an early correctness issue in the public BAdam release; if true and the pinned commit predates the fix, results would be invalid until re-pinned (Gate G4 exists to detect this).", "Actual MathInstruct-to-benchmark overlap rates are unmeasured; if retention falls below the 0.80 floor the primary population reverts to the full test set and contamination must be disclosed in the headline.", "Serving throughput for 70B greedy decoding (1.4-12+ node-hours across configurations) may exceed budget and truncate the exploratory battery or the few-shot sensitivity arm.", "Licensing/download feasibility for Llama 3-70B weights and MathInstruct in any executing environment is unconfirmed; no execution has occurred or is claimed.", "Sensitivity to sequence length (2k vs 4k), packing policy, and gradient-checkpointing granularity is unquantified and can shift both the accuracy and the memory conclusions.", "Whether reviewers treat cost metrics as purely descriptive (as designed) or expect them to enter the decision rule; the quadrant scheme keeps the two separate either way."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9e9ea44fa1678f9d", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-9e9ea44fa1678f9d", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"answer\": \"# EXPERIMENT DESIGN \\u2014 BAdam vs LoRA on Llama 3 70B + MathInstruct, zero-shot math transfer\\n\\n**Status / provenance.** Design-only artifact. Nothing was trained, downloaded, or measured. Every number below is either (i) a *direct sandbox probe* (E-numbered, in Evidence), or (ii) **analytic arithmetic** under explicitly declared assumptions (T-numbered). No GPU was used. Citations are limited to mechanism-level attribution (author/venue/year), with version-dependent details flagged unverified.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Question, hypotheses, endpoints\\n\\n**Primary question.** Does BAdam beat LoRA on zero-shot math when finetuning Llama 3 70B base on MathInstruct?\\n\\n- **H1 (accuracy, primary, parameter-matched):** pooled zero-shot accuracy of BAdam > LoRA.\\n- **H2 (corroboration, CO-PRIMARY SECONDARY, update-volume-matched):** under equal *active-parameter-update volume*, BAdam \\u2265 LoRA.\\n- **H3 (cost, descriptive only, never a claim of \\\"better performance\\\"):** BAdam uses less peak memory and/or more tokens/s at equal parameter budget.\\n\\n**Endpoints.** Primary: `A_pooled = mean(GSM8K acc, MATH acc)` per run on the pre-registered test population (\\u00a75). Co-primary secondary: same quantity in the update-matched regime. Descriptive: component-wise memory, throughput, wall-clock, storage/IO, extraction- and format-failure rates; exploratory benchmarks.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Arms and configurations\\n\\n| Arm | Method | Rank / modules | Active fraction per step | Role |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| A | LoRA (Hu et al., 2021) | r=16, all 7 linears/block (q,k,v,o,gate,up,down), \\u03b1=32 | 1 (all params) | Primary baseline |\\n| B-hi / B-md / B-lo | BAdam (Sun et al., ICLR 2024) | identical to A | \\u03c1 = 1/4, 1/8, 1/16 | Treatment, \\u03c1 grid |\\n| A-lr | LoRA | r=16 | 1 | LR grid (\\u00bd\\u00d7, 1\\u00d7, 2\\u00d7) |\\n| B-um | BAdam, update-matched | identical to A | \\u03c1_um = **1/2** (largest in grid; cost reason \\u00a78) | Co-primary secondary |\\n| C0 | Frozen base | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | Reference / floor |\\n| A64, B64 | LoRA / BAdam | r=64 | 1 / \\u03c1_md | Sensitivity |\\n\\n**Mechanism attributed to BAdam** (defensible level): a LoRA-style low-rank reparameterisation of the weight update on frozen base weights trained by **blocked block-coordinate descent** \\u2014 low-rank parameters are partitioned into blocks and only one block (an \\u03c1 fraction of the low-rank parameters) is activated and Adam-updated per step. The claimed memory/throughput advantage additionally requires that **inactive blocks are excluded from the backward graph**; this is the design's central falsifiable engineering assumption, hence Gate G3.\\n\\n**\\u03c1 is a declared hyperparameter, not a constant.** Pre-registered grid \\u03c1 \\u2208 {1/16, 1/8, 1/4}; total steps are chosen so an integer number of complete block cycles fits in the run (coverage fairness: every low-rank parameter receives the same number of update slots by construction). Headline setting, in strict precedence: (1) the reference implementation/paper default *if verifiable against the source*; else (2) the knee of the accuracy\\u2013memory Pareto curve computed **only on a MathInstruct development slice** (2,000 held-out pairs; training loss + extractable-answer rate on held-out MathInstruct items); **never** on any benchmark. The full \\u03c1 curve, not a single point, is the methodological artifact for the cost axis.\\n\\n### 2.1 Mechanism-fidelity probe \\u2014 HARD GATE (not a footnote)\\nRun at Tier 0 before any 70B spend.\\n- **G1 active-parameter census:** instrumented per-step count of parameters receiving a nonzero update; must satisfy |active|/|trainable| = \\u03c1 \\u00b1 1 %, with uniform block coverage over a cycle (\\u03c7\\u00b2 check).\\n- **G2 optimizer-state allocation:** Adam moment buffers must exist only for active blocks (asserted on allocation records, not on reported totals).\\n- **G3 backward-graph truncation (the decisive one):** at identical micro-batch, sequence length and gradient-checkpointing, compare (i) peak activation memory and (ii) autograd-node / backward-kernel counts across \\u03c1 = 1, 1/2, 1/8, 1/16. Both must scale with the number of layers holding active blocks. **If G3 fails the memory/throughput hypothesis is untestable as specified**: the study is reported as a *sparsity-of-updates* accuracy comparison with an explicit mechanism-fidelity-failure statement, and no cost claim of any kind is permitted.\\n- **G4 gradient-consistency sanity:** with \\u03c1 = 1, BAdam must reproduce LoRA's loss curve within tolerance (same seed, same data order). Failure \\u21d2 the implementation is not a superset of LoRA and the comparison is invalid; halt and re-pin.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Controls (all pre-registered; the only intended difference is the update rule)\\n\\nIdentical across arms: base checkpoint hash; tokenizer/chat template; train split and per-seed shuffled order (seed-derived permutation shared across arms); sequence packing and drop-last policy; max seq len (2 048 default, 4 096 sensitivity); global effective batch in tokens; AdamW (\\u03b21=0.9, \\u03b22=0.95, wd=0, \\u03b5=1e-8); LR peak and cosine shape with 3 % warmup; epoch/step count; loss mask (prompt masked, solution supervised); no resampling/filtering; bf16 autocast, TF32 on, FlashAttention on, **identical gradient-checkpointing**; identical FSDP/ZeRO-3 sharding plan, node count, micro-batch, storage backend and page-cache state. Fairness is further protected by declaring \\\"best LR per arm within the declared \\u00bd\\u00d7/1\\u00d7/2\\u00d7 grid, selected on the MathInstruct dev slice only\\\" as the comparison rule, so neither arm is strawmanned. No early stopping; the final checkpoint is the evaluated artifact (fixed-interval checkpoints give descriptive curves only). Eval logs are hash-logged to evidence the no-peeking rule.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Data and contamination protocol (pre-registered fallback)\\n\\nMathInstruct (~240k pairs) is the only training data; benchmarks are read-only, checksummed, never used for LR selection, checkpoint selection, or stopping.\\n\\n**Detection (run before training, frozen):** normalise questions (lowercase, strip punctuation, mask numerals), then flag a test item as *leaked-adjacent* if (i) exact match to a MathInstruct question, or (ii) \\u2265 0.85 masked-5-gram containment (sensitivity threshold 0.60). Report overlap rates per benchmark; these are **unknown until measured**. Note: MathInstruct is reported to derive partly from GSM8K/MATH **train** sources (recollection, to verify), so test leakage should be near-duplicate rather than split inversion.\\n\\n**Pre-registered population rule (the tightened item):**\\n- Primary population = **decontaminated** subset **iff** retained fraction \\u2265 **0.80 on both** GSM8K and MATH **and** n_GSM8K \\u2265 1 050 and n_MATH \\u2265 4 000.\\n- Otherwise the primary population **reverts to the full test set**, with the overlap rate disclosed in the headline table and the decontaminated analysis demoted to a sensitivity analysis.\\n- Rationale (T5): with paired item discordance p_d, the pooled endpoint's item-level 95 % half-width is `0.5\\u00b7\\u221a(p_d/n_g + p_d/n_m)`. At p_d = 0.15 the full sets already give \\u22481.17 pt, so the \\u00b10.5 pt MRD cannot absorb aggressive filtering; the 0.80 / 1 050 / 4 000 thresholds keep the item half-width \\u2264 ~1.5 pt even under the pessimistic p_d = 0.20.\\n\\n**The logic that actually protects the comparison:** both arms share the same base model and the same data, so leakage inflates *both*. The asymmetric risk is differential memorisation (a sparsely-updated model may memorise leaked items less), which biases **against BAdam**. Therefore report \\u0394-from-base alongside arm-vs-arm, and report the arm difference separately on leaked-adjacent vs clean items; if the arms differ only on leaked-adjacent items, interpret as memorisation, not reasoning.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Evaluation protocol\\n\\n**Zero-shot (primary), stated to remove ambiguity:** one fixed instruction template, **zero exemplars**, no CoT trigger beyond the neutral instruction, greedy decoding (T=0; no sampling, no self-consistency, no calculator/tool use, no answer-checker reranking), max 1 024 new tokens.\\n**Few-shot (4- and 8-shot, fixed exemplar sets, identical across arms) is a floor-artefact sensitivity only** \\u2014 reported, never used for the headline claim or for selection.\\n\\n**Primary benchmarks:** GSM8K test (1 319), MATH test (5 000). MATH500 is a development subset **only** (never for selection or headline reporting). **Exploratory:** GSM-Hard, SVAMP, MultiArith, MMLU-STEM/math, AQuA, TheoremQA.\\n\\n**Scoring (frozen before any arm is scored):** deterministic two-stage \\u2014 (1) format-aware extraction (last `\\\\boxed{...}` \\u2192 \\\"final answer is/=\\\" \\u2192 last numeric expression); (2) mathematical-equity grading by symbolic normalisation (sympy-style), string match for MCQ. **Extraction failures are counted incorrect** and reported as a per-arm rate beside accuracy, because differential format breakdown is itself an outcome. The scorer is frozen, versioned, and unit-tested against 200 hand-labelled generations (Gate G-B requires \\u2265 95 % agreement) before any arm is scored.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Cost metrics (descriptive, first-class)\\n\\nPer arm, per seed, instrumented: peak per-GPU memory decomposed into **weights / activations / gradients / optimizer state**; steady-state tokens/s (excluding first 5 % of steps); step time; wall-clock to the accuracy endpoint; GPU-hours; checkpoint bytes (adapter vs optimizer state); IO volume and dataloader wait fraction; **eval-serving cost as its own line item** (\\u00a78). Memory deltas are reported *only with* component attribution \\u2014 an unattributed memory delta is not reported. Gradient checkpointing is a declared constant, not a free variable, because it dominates peak memory.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Statistical analysis plan and honest power\\n\\n**Estimator (primary, pre-registered):** seed-level paired analysis \\u2014 \\u0394_s = A_pooled(BAdam,s) \\u2212 A_pooled(LoRA,s) over matched seeds; mean \\u0394\\u0302 with a t-based 95 % CI on k\\u22121 df, plus a mixed-effects logistic model on item-level correctness (fixed: arm, benchmark, regime; random: seed, item) as model-based confirmation. **Item-level bootstrap CIs are secondary and explicitly labelled as understating uncertainty**, since they treat training as deterministic; McNemar/permutation tests are descriptive only.\\n\\n**Seed-count honesty (T4; no claim that k=3 suffices).** Seed-term CI half-width = `t_{k\\u22121}\\u00b7\\u015d_run/\\u221ak`; a superiority claim over MRD = 0.5 pt needs \\u0394\\u0302 > 0.5 + that half-width:\\n\\n| \\u015d_run (pooled endpoint) | k=3 | k=4 | k=5 | k=7 |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 0.8 pt | 2.49 | 1.77 | **1.49** | 1.24 |\\n| 1.2 pt | 3.48 | 2.41 | **1.99** | 1.61 |\\n| 2.0 pt | 5.47 | 3.68 | **2.98** | 2.35 |\\n\\n**Second, harder limit (T5):** the *item-level* term is fixed by test-set size and observed discordance and is **not reduced by more seeds**: 95 % half-width 0.68 pt at p_d=0.05, 0.96 at 0.10, 1.36 at 0.20, 1.66 at 0.30. **Consequence stated up front:** on the full GSM8K+MATH pair a 0.5 pt MRD is unreachable unless p_d \\u22728 % *and* \\u015d_run \\u22720.8 pt. Handling: p_d and \\u015d_run are estimated **only** from Tier 0 (8B) and the frozen-base-vs-LoRA disagreement pattern, locked before 70B outcomes are unblinded; the report states the *achieved resolution* and labels the verdict at that resolution. The MRD is never widened post hoc to manufacture significance.\\n\\n**Seed-count decision rule.** k = 5 preferred (gate G-C); k = 3 permitted only as minimum-viable and then reported as \\\"powered to resolve ~3\\u20135 pt; a null is not evidence of equivalence\\\". One **arm-blind, variance-based** seed addition is permitted if Tier 1 yields \\u015d_run > 1.5 pt.\\n\\n**Decision rule.**\\n1. Claim \\\"BAdam better on zero-shot math\\\" only if (a) the 95 % CI lower bound of \\u0394\\u0302 exceeds MRD (0.5 pt, or the locked achieved resolution if larger), **and** (b) direction is consistent in \\u2265 \\u23082k/3\\u2309+1... i.e. all 3 if k=3 and \\u22654 of 5, **and** (c) **H2 corroboration:** the update-matched arm shows \\u0394_um \\u2265 0 (CI upper bound above \\u22121 pt), i.e. the parameter-matched result is *reproduced or mechanistically explained*.\\n2. **Mandatory interpretation:** a parameter-matched deficit with update-matched parity is **evidence of undertraining from the reduced per-step update budget, not of a harmful sparsity mechanism**, and must not be written as \\\"BAdam is worse\\\".\\n3. Equivalence band: CI inside \\u00b1resolution \\u21d2 \\\"undetermined at this resolution\\\", never \\\"LoRA is better\\\".\\n4. Cost is descriptive; a memory/throughput win is reported in quadrant form and never as \\\"better performance\\\".\\n5. Holm correction across 2 primary benchmarks \\u00d7 2 regimes; exploratory benchmarks labelled exploratory.\\n\\n**Quadrant scheme:** accuracy-win+cost-win / accuracy-win+cost-neutral-or-worse / accuracy-null+cost-win / accuracy-null+cost-neutral, each with a pre-written sentence (\\u00a79).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Feasibility \\u2014 tiered, with gates and scaling arithmetic\\n\\nNo GPU class, node count or GPU-hour envelope is available to me (E3/E4: no exposed GPU, no DNS), so the T-numbers use *declared, unconfirmed* placeholders that **must be replaced by Tier-0 measured values**; nothing here assumes a specific cluster.\\n\\n**Scaling formulas.** Train: `node-h \\u2248 4\\u00b7P\\u00b7T_tok / (N_gpu\\u00b7peak\\u00b7MFU_train\\u00b73600)`. Eval: `node-h \\u2248 2\\u00b7P\\u00b7T_gen / (N_gpu\\u00b7peak\\u00b7MFU_decode\\u00b73600)`, cross-checked against generated-token volume \\u00f7 aggregate tokens/s.\\n\\n**Tier 0 \\u2014 8B plumbing + fidelity (no 70B spend).** Llama-3-8B, identical recipe, all code paths, G1\\u2013G4, scorer unit test, first p_d and \\u015d_run estimates, LR smoke test, memory-scaling sanity. Analytic cost ~2.8 node-hours training + ~0.2 eval on an 8\\u00d7H100-class node.\\n- **Gate G-A (fidelity):** G1\\u2013G4 pass \\u2192 proceed. Fail on G3 \\u2192 proceed **accuracy-only** with a mechanism-fidelity-failure label and no cost claims. Fail on G4 \\u2192 halt, re-pin implementation.\\n- **Gate G-B (scorer):** \\u226595 % agreement with 200 hand labels \\u2192 freeze eval harness. Fail \\u2192 fix scorer; score nothing.\\n\\n**Tier 1 \\u2014 minimum-viable 70B.** Arms A + B-md only, headline LR, single \\u03c1, **k=2 seeds** for variance/memory estimation (not for claims). Analytic: ~4.1 h per 70B epoch on 8\\u00d7H100-class at MFU 0.40; ~15 h on A100-class at MFU 0.35.\\n- **Gate G-C:** runs complete without divergence and \\u015d_run is estimated \\u2192 **unlocks k \\u2208 {3,5,7}** via \\u00a77 and the \\u03c1_um choice.\\n- **Gate G-D (power reality check):** compare locked achieved resolution with the MRD; if resolution > 1.5 pt the study is flagged *before unblinding* as unable to certify a 0.5 pt difference, and this is written into the report.\\n\\n**Tier 2 \\u2014 full design.** k seeds; parameter-matched arms; update-matched arm B-um; \\u03c1 \\u2208 {1/16,1/8,1/4} curve; r=64 sensitivity; LR grid; few-shot sensitivity; exploratory battery.\\n\\n**Cost matrix (analytic estimates, NOT measurements; MFU and decode efficiency are assumptions).**\\n\\n| Configuration | Training node-h | Eval node-h | Eval share |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 8\\u00d7H100-class, k=3, 1 epoch, 2 arms | 24.8 | 3.9 (MFU_dec 0.25) \\u2026 9.8 (0.10) | 14\\u201328 % |\\n| 8\\u00d7H100-class, k=5 | 41.3 | 6.2 \\u2026 15.5 | 13\\u201327 % |\\n| 8\\u00d7A100-class, k=3 | 89.7 | 12.4 \\u2026 31.0 | 12\\u201326 % |\\n| 8\\u00d7A100-class, k=5 | 149.6 | 19.6 \\u2026 49.0 | 12\\u201325 % |\\n| Tier 0 (8B) | ~2.8 | ~0.2 | ~7 % |\\n\\n**Eval-serving is a first-class line item (T3):** the zero-shot battery generates \\u22489.5 M tokens per model (22.4 M with the 4/8-shot sensitivity) \\u2014 \\u22480.16 B generated tokens across 7 models (2 arms \\u00d7 3 seeds + base), i.e. 0.59 of one 70B training epoch in FLOPs and 1.4\\u201312 node-hours depending on serving efficiency. **Decode-bound serving can therefore consume a quarter to a third of the total budget**, and is budgeted explicitly rather than discovered late; note that 70B bf16 weights (~140 GB) force a multi-GPU serving replica for evaluation regardless of arm.\\n**Update-matched cost law (T6):** `C_match \\u2248 C_LoRA \\u00b7 (1/\\u03c1) \\u00b7 \\u03c4`, \\u03c4 = BAdam step time / LoRA step time. At \\u03c1 = 1/8 with \\u03c4 \\u2265 0.5 this is \\u22654\\u00d7 LoRA, which is why \\u03c1_um = 1/2 is pre-registered; this arm doubles as the honest compute-matched comparison and directly tests the efficiency claim.\\n**Storage:** base weights 140 GB per run; r=16 adapter 417 MB bf16 plus ~2.5 GB fp32 master + moments; r=64 optimizer moments 6.63 GB all-active vs 0.83 GB at 1/8 active (T7) \\u2014 the reason any large memory win must be attributed to activations/backprop, and hence why G3 is a gate.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Results / reporting template (null and memory-only outcomes reportable by construction)\\n\\n```\\nT1 Accuracy (primary): arm \\u00d7 seed \\u00d7 {GSM8K, MATH} accuracy, extraction-failure %,\\n   format-failure %, \\u0394-from-base; full and decontaminated populations labelled.\\nT2 Co-primary secondary: parameter-matched vs update-matched \\u0394\\u0302 with 95 % CI.\\nT3 Cost (descriptive): peak memory by component, tokens/s, step time, wall-clock,\\n   GPU-h, checkpoint bytes, IO volume, EVAL-SERVING node-hours.\\nT4 Mechanism fidelity: G1 active fraction (target vs measured), G2 Adam-state\\n   allocation, G3 activation-memory/backprop-node scaling vs \\u03c1, G4 LoRA-equivalence.\\nT5 Contamination: overlap rate per benchmark, retained n, population decision taken.\\nT6 \\u03c1-Pareto: (\\u03c1, accuracy, peak memory, tokens/s) \\u2014 the methodological artifact.\\nF1 Loss/accuracy-vs-step curves, both arms, all seeds.  F2 Pareto frontier.\\n```\\nPre-written verdict sentences:\\n- **Q1 accuracy-win + cost-win:** \\\"BAdam improved zero-shot math accuracy by \\u0394\\u0302 = ___ pt (95 % CI ___, ___), exceeding the pre-registered ___ pt threshold, corroborated in the update-matched regime (\\u0394_um = ___). It also used ___ % less peak memory at equal parameter budget.\\\"\\n- **Q2 accuracy-win + cost-neutral/worse:** \\\"Accuracy improved by \\u0394\\u0302 = ___ (CI ___), corroborated/uncorroborated by the update-matched arm; the memory/throughput advantage did not materialise (\\u0394mem = ___ %, \\u0394tokens/s = ___ %), so BAdam's cost rationale is not supported here.\\\"\\n- **Q3 accuracy-null + cost-win:** \\\"No accuracy difference was detectable at the achieved resolution of ___ pt (\\u0394\\u0302 = ___, CI ___). BAdam used ___ % less peak memory. **Cost reduction was observed; better performance was not.**\\\"\\n- **Q4 accuracy-null + cost-neutral:** \\\"Neither hypothesis was supported at the achieved resolution; the arms are statistically indistinguishable and comparable in cost.\\\"\\n- **Undertraining pattern (mandatory wording when it occurs):** \\\"Parameter-matched BAdam trails LoRA by ___ pt while the update-matched arm reaches parity (\\u0394_um = ___). This is evidence that the deficit follows from the reduced number of active-parameter updates, not from a harmful sparsity mechanism; it is not evidence that BAdam is worse.\\\"\\n- **Fidelity failure:** \\\"Instrumentation showed backward-graph truncation was not realised (T4). The memory/throughput hypothesis could not be tested; only a sparsity-of-updates accuracy comparison is reported.\\\"\\n- **Contamination fallback:** \\\"Overlap removal would have retained only ___ % of items, below the pre-registered 80 % floor; the primary population is therefore the full test set with overlap rate ___ % disclosed.\\\"\\n- **Seed insufficiency:** \\\"With k = ___ and \\u015d_run = ___ pt this study resolves differences of \\u2248___ pt; a null is a statement about resolution, not equivalence.\\\"\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Assumption A-list, with breakage mode\\n\\n| # | Assumption (NOT an established fact) | How this design breaks if wrong |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| A1 | Llama 3 70B **base** is the starting point | With Instruct, absolute accuracies and prompt/format handling change; the arm-vs-arm contrast likely transfers but the base-reference floor and extraction profile do not. |\\n| A2 | \\\"Zero-shot\\\" = 0 exemplars, greedy, no CoT trigger, no tools/self-consistency | A different convention (0-shot-CoT, report-style prompts) changes the metric; conclusions may not transfer. |\\n| A3 | BAdam = low-rank update + blocked BCD with inactive blocks **excluded from backprop** (Sun et al., ICLR 2024). **Unverified recollection:** the public BAdam repository reportedly had an early correctness issue fixed after release \\u2014 *unverified, to be checked against the pinned source, never used as a finding.* | If backprop is not truncated, the memory/throughput hypothesis is untestable \\u2192 G3 fails, cost section void. If the pinned implementation differs from the paper, G4 fails \\u2192 comparison invalid until re-pinned. This is exactly why the fidelity probe is a gate. |\\n| A4 | Parameter-matched primary; update-matched corroborates | If the community norm is compute-matched primary, the primary/secondary labels invert and the H1 claim loses standing. |\\n| A5 | MathInstruct = full ~240k pairs, 1 epoch, no resampling | Fewer tokens/epoch scales all costs down but may leave both arms undertrained, weakening any null conclusion. |\\n| A6 | Only linear-projection adapters; no IA\\u00b3/prefix/DoRA/full-finetune arm | A reviewer can argue the baseline is weak relative to stronger adapters; no data here answer that. |\\n| A7 | GSM8K+MATH primary; MATH500 dev-only | A different required benchmark list changes the endpoint post hoc \\u2014 a pre-registration violation. |\\n| A8 | \\u03c1 headline from paper default, else MathInstruct-dev knee | Choosing \\u03c1 using any benchmark would inflate one arm and void the comparison. |\\n| A9 | MFU 0.35\\u20130.40 training, decode efficiency 0.10\\u20130.25 | All node-hour figures shift proportionally; an under-budgeted eval phase can stop Tier 2 mid-sweep. |\\n| A10 | Weights and MathInstruct are obtainable/licensable in the executing environment | Nothing runs; the design stays unexecuted (correctly \\u2014 nothing has run here). |\\n| A11 | \\u015d_run \\u22721 pt, p_d \\u22720.10\\u20130.20 | If larger, the locked resolution exceeds the 0.5 pt MRD and the study can only report equivalence/undetermined \\u2014 by design, not failure. |\\n| A12 | Gradient checkpointing and sharding identical across arms | Otherwise memory comparisons are meaningless. |\\n| A13 | Symbolic-equivalence scoring \\u2248 human grading | Systematic extraction bias would distort accuracy and differentially penalise one arm's output style; mitigated by Gate G-B and per-arm extraction-failure reporting. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. External-validity note (practitioner starting from Instruct)\\nA practitioner will almost always start from `Llama-3-70B-Instruct`. There, absolute accuracies are far higher, so the zero-shot floor-artefact risk largely disappears and the few-shot sensitivity arm matters less; the input distribution is already chat-formatted, so the adapter's job is narrower and lower-capacity, which plausibly **shrinks** any capacity-limited gap between arms and compresses both toward a ceiling \\u2014 while the cost axis is unchanged or worse, since chat templates are longer. Two effects could flip the practical conclusion: an instruct model has fewer headroom points, making a null the expected outcome, and finetuning can *degrade* an already-strong instruct model, promoting extraction/format-failure rate from diagnostic to first-order outcome. This design's arm-vs-arm contrast should transfer qualitatively, but its absolute accuracies, the appropriateness of its 0.5 pt MRD, and its null-vs-regression reading should not be quoted for an instruct starting point without a dedicated run.\\n\\n## 12. Citation defensibility\\nMechanism-level attribution only: **LoRA** (Hu et al., 2021) \\u2014 frozen base weights plus trainable low-rank pairs on selected linear projections, all trainable parameters updated every step. **BAdam** (Sun et al., ICLR 2024) \\u2014 blocked block-coordinate descent over low-rank parameters, a subset activated per step, advertised for memory-efficient finetuning of large models. I verified **no** repository, paper text, hyperparameter table, or default \\u03c1 in this environment; every version-dependent detail (default block ratio, \\u03b1 convention, whether backprop is truncated, the state of the early public release) must be checked against the pinned source before Gate G-A and is flagged unverified in A3.\\n\\n## 13. Pre-registration artifacts to freeze before any run\\nConfig bundle + hashes; \\u03c1 grid and headline-selection rule; LR grid and dev-slice selection rule; seed list; contamination thresholds and population rule; scorer version + 200-item hand-label set; analysis script with locked estimators, MRD/resolution procedure and quadrant sentences; fidelity-gate pass criteria \\u2014 all frozen before 70B outcomes are unblinded.\", \"evidence\": \"**E \\u2014 direct sandbox probes (exploratory environment checks only; no experiment, no training, no download)**\\n\\n- **E1 Workspace is empty of research inputs.** `ls -la /workspace` and `find /workspace -maxdepth 3` \\u2192 single file `task.json` (613 B). `cat /workspace/task.json` contains only the question, \\\"Do not claim that an experiment was run\\\", and `documents.track.md: \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"` No repository, no source code, no data.\\n- **E2 No ML stack, no datasets, no reference implementations.** `python3 -V` \\u2192 3.14.4. `import torch`, `import transformers`, `import peft` \\u2192 all `ModuleNotFoundError`. `pip list` shows only system packages. `find / -maxdepth 3` for `*mathinstruct*`, `*llama*3*`, `*badam*`, `*lora*` \\u2192 no matches. No `~/.cache` and no `~/.cache/huggingface`. **MathInstruct, Llama 3-70B weights, and any BAdam/LoRA reference implementation are NOT locally available; nothing was downloaded or run.**\\n- **E3 No usable GPU.** `/usr/bin/nvidia-smi` exists but prints \\\"NVIDIA-SMI has failed because it couldn't communicate with the NVIDIA driver\\\"; `ls /dev/nvidia*` \\u2192 no device nodes (only `/dev/nvidia-caps`); `lspci` lists host GB100-class 3D controllers that are **not exposed** into this bubblewrap sandbox; task spec states `gpu_enabled: false`.\\n- **E4 No network.** `socket.create_connection(('pypi.org',443))` \\u2192 `socket.gaierror: Temporary failure in name resolution`. Consistent with `network: disabled`; no install or download possible. (Python 3.14 is a secondary practical blocker for CUDA wheels.)\\n- **E5 Irrelevant host resources.** `nproc` = 224; `free -g` \\u2248 2011 GB RAM; `/` and `/tmp` are tmpfs ~1006 GB with 0 used. CPU/RAM cannot substitute for the accelerator training requires.\\n\\n**T \\u2014 analytic arithmetic (formulas + inputs stated; these are estimates, NOT measurements)**\\n\\n- **T1 Training volume.** 240 000 samples \\u00d7 ~700 tokens = 1.68\\u00d710\\u2078 tokens/epoch; 4\\u00b7P\\u00b7T with P=70\\u00d710\\u2079 \\u2192 **4.704\\u00d710\\u00b9\\u2079 FLOPs per 70B epoch** \\u2192 ~4.1 h on an 8\\u00d7H100-class node (peak 989 TFLOP/s/GPU, MFU 0.40) and ~15.0 h on an 8\\u00d7A100-80G-class node (312 TFLOP/s, MFU 0.35). k seeds \\u00d7 2 arms: k=3 \\u2192 24.8 node-h (H100-class) / 89.7 (A100-class); k=5 \\u2192 41.3 / 149.6.\\n- **T2 Decode/eval model.** Generation FLOPs = 2\\u00b7P\\u00b7(prompt+output tokens) per item; zero-shot battery (MATH 5000, GSM8K 1319, GSM-Hard 1319, SVAMP 1000, MultiArith 600, MMLU-math 1800, AQuA 254, TheoremQA 800) = 1.757\\u00d710\\u00b9\\u2078 FLOPs per model; adding 4- and 8-shot on the two primaries = 3.993\\u00d710\\u00b9\\u2078 per model; \\u00d77 models (2 arms \\u00d7 3 seeds + frozen base) = 2.795\\u00d710\\u00b9\\u2079.\\n- **T3 Eval-serving as a first-class cost.** = 0.59 of one 70B training epoch in FLOPs. Token-volume cross-check: 9.49 M generated tokens per model zero-shot, 22.43 M with the few-shot sensitivity, 0.157 B across 7 models \\u2192 1.4\\u20135.5 node-hours at 4000\\u20131000 aggregate tokens/s/GPU on 8 GPUs; FLOPs-based with MFU_dec 0.25 \\u2192 3.9 (H100-class) / 12.4 (A100-class), and 9.8 / 31.0 at MFU_dec 0.10. Eval share of the total budget: 12\\u201328 %. Raising max_new_tokens 1024\\u21922048 multiplies long-output sets by ~1.67. Serving needs \\u2265140 GB of bf16 weights \\u2192 multi-GPU replica irrespective of arm.\\n- **T4 Seed sizing (used in \\u00a77 table).** Seed-term half-width = t_{k\\u22121}\\u00b7\\u015d_run/\\u221ak (t\\u208ddf2\\u208e=4.303, df3=3.182, df4=2.776, df6=2.447, df8=2.365). For \\u015d_run=0.8/1.2/2.0 pt and k=3/4/5/7/9 the smallest claimable \\u0394\\u0302 over a 0.5 pt MRD ranges 1.13\\u21925.47 pt; e.g. \\u015d_run=1.2: k=3 \\u2192 3.48 pt, k=5 \\u2192 1.99 pt, k=7 \\u2192 1.61 pt.\\n- **T5 Item-level floor and contamination power.** Paired item SE on the pooled endpoint = 0.5\\u00b7\\u221a(p_d/n_g + p_d/n_m) with n_g=1319, n_m=5000 \\u2192 half-width 0.68 pt (p_d=0.05), 0.96 (0.10), 1.17 (0.15), 1.36 (0.20), 1.66 (0.30). Filtering both sets to retained fraction f: at p_d=0.20, f=0.8 \\u2192 1.52 pt, f=0.6 \\u2192 1.75, f=0.4 \\u2192 2.15; at p_d=0.10, f=0.8 \\u2192 1.07, f=0.6 \\u2192 1.24, f=0.4 \\u2192 1.52. This is the arithmetic basis for the pre-registered \\u22650.80 retention / n_GSM8K \\u2265 1050 / n_MATH \\u2265 4000 floor (\\u2264~1.5 pt half-width even at p_d=0.20) and the revert-to-full-set fallback.\\n- **T6 Update-matching cost law.** C_match \\u2248 C_LoRA \\u00b7 (1/\\u03c1) \\u00b7 \\u03c4 with \\u03c4 = BAdam step time / LoRA step time; at \\u03c1=1/8, \\u03c4\\u22650.5 \\u21d2 \\u22654\\u00d7 LoRA cost \\u2014 the stated reason \\u03c1_um = 1/2 is pre-registered for the co-primary secondary arm.\\n- **T7 Parameter/memory arithmetic from Llama-3-70B geometry** (80 layers, d=8192, kv-head total dim 1024, ffn 28672, 7 target linears/block; per-block \\u03a3(in+out)=162 832): r=8 \\u2192 103.5 M trainable (0.148 %), r=16 \\u2192 207.1 M (0.296 %), r=32 \\u2192 414.2 M (0.592 %), r=64 \\u2192 828.4 M (1.183 %) of 70 B. All-active fp32 Adam moments at r=64 \\u2248 6.63 GB vs \\u22480.83 GB at 1/8 active fraction; bf16 70B weights = 140 GB; r=16 adapter = 417 MB bf16, +2.5 GB fp32 master + moments. Because optimizer-state savings are single-digit GB, any large memory win must come from activation/backprop truncation \\u2014 which is why G3 is a hard gate rather than a footnote.\\n- **T8 Tier-0 pilot sizing.** 8B training scales by P: 3 seeds \\u00d7 2 arms \\u00d7 1 epoch \\u2248 2.83 node-h and \\u22480.2 node-h eval on an 8\\u00d7H100-class node (same formulas as T1/T2 with P=8\\u00d710\\u2079); 70B/8B compute ratio \\u2248 8.75\\u00d7.\\n\\n**Repository observation.** There is no repository to integrate with: the entire task surface is a single 613-byte JSON containing the question and a design-only note. Consequently the design specifies its own toolchain requirements (pinned BAdam and PEFT implementations, FSDP/ZeRO-3 trainer, vLLM/HF-serving-style batched greedy decoding for eval) as artifacts to be created and hash-frozen at Tier 0, rather than modifications to existing code \\u2014 and every implementation-dependent quantity (default \\u03c1, \\u03b1 convention, backprop truncation) is deferred to the pinned-source verification step instead of being asserted.\", \"assumptions\": [\"A1 The finetuning starting point is the Llama 3 70B **base** checkpoint (design directive); with an Instruct start, absolute accuracies, prompt handling and the null-vs-regression reading change (\\u00a711).\", \"A2 'Zero-shot' is fixed as: zero in-context exemplars, one neutral instruction, greedy decoding, no CoT trigger, no tools, no self-consistency, no answer-checker reranking.\", \"A3 BAdam is attributed a LoRA-style low-rank update trained by blocked block-coordinate descent with an \\u03c1 fraction of low-rank parameters active per step, and \\u2014 the load-bearing engineering assumption \\u2014 inactive blocks excluded from the backward graph. The recollection of an early post-release correctness fix in the public BAdam repo is explicitly UNVERIFIED and is to be checked against the pinned source; it is tied to gates G3/G4 and is never used as a finding.\", \"A4 Parameter-matched is the primary comparison; update-volume-matched is the co-primary secondary that must corroborate or explain the primary.\", \"A5 MathInstruct is used as ~240k pairs for 1 epoch with no resampling or filtering; the token-volume arithmetic scales linearly if this changes.\", \"A6 Only linear-projection adapters are compared; no IA3/prefix/DoRA/full-finetune arm (full finetuning of 70B is assumed out of budget).\", \"A7 GSM8K test + MATH test are the primary zero-shot endpoints, MATH500 is development-only, and GSM-Hard/SVAMP/MultiArith/MMLU-math/AQuA/TheoremQA are exploratory.\", \"A8 No GPU class, node count, MFU or GPU-hour envelope is assumed: all cost figures are parameterised by (GPU class x count x MFU) placeholders to be replaced by Tier-0 measurements.\", \"A9 Training and decode MFU placeholders are 0.35-0.40 and 0.10-0.25 respectively; average sample length ~700 tokens; average prompt ~200-350 tokens with 1024-token generation caps.\", \"A10 Llama 3-70B weights and MathInstruct are obtainable and licensable in some hypothetical executing environment; nothing is available or downloaded here.\", \"A11 Achievable statistical resolution is treated as an empirical quantity estimated only from Tier 0/Tier 1 (arm-blind) and locked before 70B outcomes are unblinded; the 0.5 pt MRD is never widened post hoc.\", \"A12 Gradient checkpointing, sharding plan, batch size in tokens and sequence length are identical across arms, so peak-memory comparisons are interpretable.\", \"A13 Symbolic-equivalence grading approximates human judgement; extraction failures count as incorrect and are reported per arm.\", \"A14 Mechanism-level attributions to Hu et al. 2021 and Sun et al. ICLR 2024 are at the level defensible from general knowledge; no paper text, repository, or hyperparameter table was inspected here.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Statistical resolution, not effort, is the binding constraint: with 1319+5000 test items the paired item-level term alone gives a ~1.0-1.7 pt half-width, so a 0.5 pt MRD may be unclaimable at ANY seed count unless observed arm discordance is low. This is disclosed pre-unblinding rather than resolved by more compute.\", \"The true run-level SD (s_run) of the pooled endpoint is unknown; if it exceeds ~1.5 pt the pre-registered arm-blind seed addition triggers and Tier 2 cost rises ~40-60%.\", \"Whether the pinned BAdam implementation actually truncates backprop for inactive blocks (Gate G3). If not, the entire cost hypothesis is untestable and the study silently becomes an accuracy-only sparsity comparison.\", \"The default per-step active-block ratio in the reference implementation, and whether the paper's reported configuration is reproducible; this determines the headline rho and therefore the headline result.\", \"The unverified recollection of an early correctness issue in the public BAdam release; if true and the pinned commit predates the fix, results would be invalid until re-pinned (Gate G4 exists to detect this).\", \"Actual MathInstruct-to-benchmark overlap rates are unmeasured; if retention falls below the 0.80 floor the primary population reverts to the full test set and contamination must be disclosed in the headline.\", \"Serving throughput for 70B greedy decoding (1.4-12+ node-hours across configurations) may exceed budget and truncate the exploratory battery or the few-shot sensitivity arm.\", \"Licensing/download feasibility for Llama 3-70B weights and MathInstruct in any executing environment is unconfirmed; no execution has occurred or is claimed.\", \"Sensitivity to sequence length (2k vs 4k), packing policy, and gradient-checkpointing granularity is unquantified and can shift both the accuracy and the memory conclusions.\", \"Whether reviewers treat cost metrics as purely descriptive (as designed) or expect them to enter the decision rule; the quadrant scheme keeps the two separate either way.\"], \"stage\": \"result\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-c1f15784-0ec1-49a8-872e-c5af123735c8", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "end_researcher_turn: {\"answer\": \"# EXPERIMENT DESIGN \\u2014 BAdam vs LoRA on Llama 3 70B + MathInstruct, zero-shot math transfer\\n\\n**Status / provenance.** Design-only artifact. Nothing was trained, downloaded, or measured. Every number below is either (i) a *direct sandbox probe* (E-numbered, in Evidence), or (ii) **analytic arithmetic** under explicitly declared assumptions (T-numbered). No GPU was used. Citations are limited to mechanism-level attribution (author/venue/year), with version-dependent details flagged unverified.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Question, hypotheses, endpoints\\n\\n**Primary question.** Does BAdam beat LoRA on zero-shot math when finetuning Llama 3 70B base on MathInstruct?\\n\\n- **H1 (accuracy, primary, parameter-matched):** pooled zero-shot accuracy of BAdam > LoRA.\\n- **H2 (corroboration, CO-PRIMARY SECONDARY, update-volume-matched):** under equal *active-parameter-update volume*, BAdam \\u2265 LoRA.\\n- **H3 (cost, descriptive only, never a claim of \\\"better performance\\\"):** BAdam uses less peak memory and/or more tokens/s at equal parameter budget.\\n\\n**Endpoints.** Primary: `A_pooled = mean(GSM8K acc, MATH acc)` per run on the pre-registered test population (\\u00a75). Co-primary secondary: same quantity in the update-matched regime. Descriptive: component-wise memory, throughput, wall-clock, storage/IO, extraction- and format-failure rates; exploratory benchmarks.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Arms and configurations\\n\\n| Arm | Method | Rank / modules | Active fraction per step | Role |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| A | LoRA (Hu et al., 2021) | r=16, all 7 linears/block (q,k,v,o,gate,up,down), \\u03b1=32 | 1 (all params) | Primary baseline |\\n| B-hi / B-md / B-lo | BAdam (Sun et al., ICLR 2024) | identical to A | \\u03c1 = 1/4, 1/8, 1/16 | Treatment, \\u03c1 grid |\\n| A-lr | LoRA | r=16 | 1 | LR grid (\\u00bd\\u00d7, 1\\u00d7, 2\\u00d7) |\\n| B-um | BAdam, update-matched | identical to A | \\u03c1_um = **1/2** (largest in grid; cost reason \\u00a78) | Co-primary secondary |\\n| C0 | Frozen base | \\u2014 | \\u2014 | Reference / floor |\\n| A64, B64 | LoRA / BAdam | r=64 | 1 / \\u03c1_md | Sensitivity |\\n\\n**Mechanism attributed to BAdam** (defensible level): a LoRA-style low-rank reparameterisation of the weight update on frozen base weights trained by **blocked block-coordinate descent** \\u2014 low-rank parameters are partitioned into blocks and only one block (an \\u03c1 fraction of the low-rank parameters) is activated and Adam-updated per step. The claimed memory/throughput advantage additionally requires that **inactive blocks are excluded from the backward graph**; this is the design's central falsifiable engineering assumption, hence Gate G3.\\n\\n**\\u03c1 is a declared hyperparameter, not a constant.** Pre-registered grid \\u03c1 \\u2208 {1/16, 1/8, 1/4}; total steps are chosen so an integer number of complete block cycles fits in the run (coverage fairness: every low-rank parameter receives the same number of update slots by construction). Headline setting, in strict precedence: (1) the reference implementation/paper default *if verifiable against the source*; else (2) the knee of the accuracy\\u2013memory Pareto curve computed **only on a MathInstruct development slice** (2,000 held-out pairs; training loss + extractable-answer rate on held-out MathInstruct items); **never** on any benchmark. The full \\u03c1 curve, not a single point, is the methodological artifact for the cost axis.\\n\\n### 2.1 Mechanism-fidelity probe \\u2014 HARD GATE (not a footnote)\\nRun at Tier 0 before any 70B spend.\\n- **G1 active-parameter census:** instrumented per-step count of parameters receiving a nonzero update; must satisfy |active|/|trainable| = \\u03c1 \\u00b1 1 %, with uniform block coverage over a cycle (\\u03c7\\u00b2 check).\\n- **G2 optimizer-state allocation:** Adam moment buffers must exist only for active blocks (asserted on allocation records, not on reported totals).\\n- **G3 backward-graph truncation (the decisive one):** at identical micro-batch, sequence length and gradient-checkpointing, compare (i) peak activation memory and (ii) autograd-node / backward-kernel counts across \\u03c1 = 1, 1/2, 1/8, 1/16. Both must scale with the number of layers holding active blocks. **If G3 fails the memory/throughput hypothesis is untestable as specified**: the study is reported as a *sparsity-of-updates* accuracy comparison with an explicit mechanism-fidelity-failure statement, and no cost claim of any kind is permitted.\\n- **G4 gradient-consistency sanity:** with \\u03c1 = 1, BAdam must reproduce LoRA's loss curve within tolerance (same seed, same data order). Failure \\u21d2 the implementation is not a superset of LoRA and the comparison is invalid; halt and re-pin.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Controls (all pre-registered; the only intended difference is the update rule)\\n\\nIdentical across arms: base checkpoint hash; tokenizer/chat template; train split and per-seed shuffled order (seed-derived permutation shared across arms); sequence packing and drop-last policy; max seq len (2 048 default, 4 096 sensitivity); global effective batch in tokens; AdamW (\\u03b21=0.9, \\u03b22=0.95, wd=0, \\u03b5=1e-8); LR peak and cosine shape with 3 % warmup; epoch/step count; loss mask (prompt masked, solution supervised); no resampling/filtering; bf16 autocast, TF32 on, FlashAttention on, **identical gradient-checkpointing**; identical FSDP/ZeRO-3 sharding plan, node count, micro-batch, storage backend and page-cache state. Fairness is further protected by declaring \\\"best LR per arm within the declared \\u00bd\\u00d7/1\\u00d7/2\\u00d7 grid, selected on the MathInstruct dev slice only\\\" as the comparison rule, so neither arm is strawmanned. No early stopping; the final checkpoint is the evaluated artifact (fixed-interval checkpoints give descriptive curves only). Eval logs are hash-logged to evidence the no-peeking rule.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Data and contamination protocol (pre-registered fallback)\\n\\nMathInstruct (~240k pairs) is the only training data; benchmarks are read-only, checksummed, never used for LR selection, checkpoint selection, or stopping.\\n\\n**Detection (run before training, frozen):** normalise questions (lowercase, strip punctuation, mask numerals), then flag a test item as *leaked-adjacent* if (i) exact match to a MathInstruct question, or (ii) \\u2265 0.85 masked-5-gram containment (sensitivity threshold 0.60). Report overlap rates per benchmark; these are **unknown until measured**. Note: MathInstruct is reported to derive partly from GSM8K/MATH **train** sources (recollection, to verify), so test leakage should be near-duplicate rather than split inversion.\\n\\n**Pre-registered population rule (the tightened item):**\\n- Primary population = **decontaminated** subset **iff** retained fraction \\u2265 **0.80 on both** GSM8K and MATH **and** n_GSM8K \\u2265 1 050 and n_MATH \\u2265 4 000.\\n- Otherwise the primary population **reverts to the full test set**, with the overlap rate disclosed in the headline table and the decontaminated analysis demoted to a sensitivity analysis.\\n- Rationale (T5): with paired item discordance p_d, the pooled endpoint's item-level 95 % half-width is `0.5\\u00b7\\u221a(p_d/n_g + p_d/n_m)`. At p_d = 0.15 the full sets already give \\u22481.17 pt, so the \\u00b10.5 pt MRD cannot absorb aggressive filtering; the 0.80 / 1 050 / 4 000 thresholds keep the item half-width \\u2264 ~1.5 pt even under the pessimistic p_d = 0.20.\\n\\n**The logic that actually protects the comparison:** both arms share the same base model and the same data, so leakage inflates *both*. The asymmetric risk is differential memorisation (a sparsely-updated model may memorise leaked items less), which biases **against BAdam**. Therefore report \\u0394-from-base alongside arm-vs-arm, and report the arm difference separately on leaked-adjacent vs clean items; if the arms differ only on leaked-adjacent items, interpret as memorisation, not reasoning.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Evaluation protocol\\n\\n**Zero-shot (primary), stated to remove ambiguity:** one fixed instruction template, **zero exemplars**, no CoT trigger beyond the neutral instruction, greedy decoding (T=0; no sampling, no self-consistency, no calculator/tool use, no answer-checker reranking), max 1 024 new tokens.\\n**Few-shot (4- and 8-shot, fixed exemplar sets, identical across arms) is a floor-artefact sensitivity only** \\u2014 reported, never used for the headline claim or for selection.\\n\\n**Primary benchmarks:** GSM8K test (1 319), MATH test (5 000). MATH500 is a development subset **only** (never for selection or headline reporting). **Exploratory:** GSM-Hard, SVAMP, MultiArith, MMLU-STEM/math, AQuA, TheoremQA.\\n\\n**Scoring (frozen before any arm is scored):** deterministic two-stage \\u2014 (1) format-aware extraction (last `\\\\boxed{...}` \\u2192 \\\"final answer is/=\\\" \\u2192 last numeric expression); (2) mathematical-equity grading by symbolic normalisation (sympy-style), string match for MCQ. **Extraction failures are counted incorrect** and reported as a per-arm rate beside accuracy, because differential format breakdown is itself an outcome. The scorer is frozen, versioned, and unit-tested against 200 hand-labelled generations (Gate G-B requires \\u2265 95 % agreement) before any arm is scored.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Cost metrics (descriptive, first-class)\\n\\nPer arm, per seed, instrumented: peak per-GPU memory decomposed into **weights / activations / gradients / optimizer state**; steady-state tokens/s (excluding first 5 % of steps); step time; wall-clock to the accuracy endpoint; GPU-hours; checkpoint bytes (adapter vs optimizer state); IO volume and dataloader wait fraction; **eval-serving cost as its own line item** (\\u00a78). Memory deltas are reported *only with* component attribution \\u2014 an unattributed memory delta is not reported. Gradient checkpointing is a declared constant, not a free variable, because it dominates peak memory.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Statistical analysis plan and honest power\\n\\n**Estimator (primary, pre-registered):** seed-level paired analysis \\u2014 \\u0394_s = A_pooled(BAdam,s) \\u2212 A_pooled(LoRA,s) over matched seeds; mean \\u0394\\u0302 with a t-based 95 % CI on k\\u22121 df, plus a mixed-effects logistic model on item-level correctness (fixed: arm, benchmark, regime; random: seed, item) as model-based confirmation. **Item-level bootstrap CIs are secondary and explicitly labelled as understating uncertainty**, since they treat training as deterministic; McNemar/permutation tests are descriptive only.\\n\\n**Seed-count honesty (T4; no claim that k=3 suffices).** Seed-term CI half-width = `t_{k\\u22121}\\u00b7\\u015d_run/\\u221ak`; a superiority claim over MRD = 0.5 pt needs \\u0394\\u0302 > 0.5 + that half-width:\\n\\n| \\u015d_run (pooled endpoint) | k=3 | k=4 | k=5 | k=7 |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 0.8 pt | 2.49 | 1.77 | **1.49** | 1.24 |\\n| 1.2 pt | 3.48 | 2.41 | **1.99** | 1.61 |\\n| 2.0 pt | 5.47 | 3.68 | **2.98** | 2.35 |\\n\\n**Second, harder limit (T5):** the *item-level* term is fixed by test-set size and observed discordance and is **not reduced by more seeds**: 95 % half-width 0.68 pt at p_d=0.05, 0.96 at 0.10, 1.36 at 0.20, 1.66 at 0.30. **Consequence stated up front:** on the full GSM8K+MATH pair a 0.5 pt MRD is unreachable unless p_d \\u22728 % *and* \\u015d_run \\u22720.8 pt. Handling: p_d and \\u015d_run are estimated **only** from Tier 0 (8B) and the frozen-base-vs-LoRA disagreement pattern, locked before 70B outcomes are unblinded; the report states the *achieved resolution* and labels the verdict at that resolution. The MRD is never widened post hoc to manufacture significance.\\n\\n**Seed-count decision rule.** k = 5 preferred (gate G-C); k = 3 permitted only as minimum-viable and then reported as \\\"powered to resolve ~3\\u20135 pt; a null is not evidence of equivalence\\\". One **arm-blind, variance-based** seed addition is permitted if Tier 1 yields \\u015d_run > 1.5 pt.\\n\\n**Decision rule.**\\n1. Claim \\\"BAdam better on zero-shot math\\\" only if (a) the 95 % CI lower bound of \\u0394\\u0302 exceeds MRD (0.5 pt, or the locked achieved resolution if larger), **and** (b) direction is consistent in \\u2265 \\u23082k/3\\u2309+1... i.e. all 3 if k=3 and \\u22654 of 5, **and** (c) **H2 corroboration:** the update-matched arm shows \\u0394_um \\u2265 0 (CI upper bound above \\u22121 pt), i.e. the parameter-matched result is *reproduced or mechanistically explained*.\\n2. **Mandatory interpretation:** a parameter-matched deficit with update-matched parity is **evidence of undertraining from the reduced per-step update budget, not of a harmful sparsity mechanism**, and must not be written as \\\"BAdam is worse\\\".\\n3. Equivalence band: CI inside \\u00b1resolution \\u21d2 \\\"undetermined at this resolution\\\", never \\\"LoRA is better\\\".\\n4. Cost is descriptive; a memory/throughput win is reported in quadrant form and never as \\\"better performance\\\".\\n5. Holm correction across 2 primary benchmarks \\u00d7 2 regimes; exploratory benchmarks labelled exploratory.\\n\\n**Quadrant scheme:** accuracy-win+cost-win / accuracy-win+cost-neutral-or-worse / accuracy-null+cost-win / accuracy-null+cost-neutral, each with a pre-written sentence (\\u00a79).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Feasibility \\u2014 tiered, with gates and scaling arithmetic\\n\\nNo GPU class, node count or GPU-hour envelope is available to me (E3/E4: no exposed GPU, no DNS), so the T-numbers use *declared, unconfirmed* placeholders that **must be replaced by Tier-0 measured values**; nothing here assumes a specific cluster.\\n\\n**Scaling formulas.** Train: `node-h \\u2248 4\\u00b7P\\u00b7T_tok / (N_gpu\\u00b7peak\\u00b7MFU_train\\u00b73600)`. Eval: `node-h \\u2248 2\\u00b7P\\u00b7T_gen / (N_gpu\\u00b7peak\\u00b7MFU_decode\\u00b73600)`, cross-checked against generated-token volume \\u00f7 aggregate tokens/s.\\n\\n**Tier 0 \\u2014 8B plumbing + fidelity (no 70B spend).** Llama-3-8B, identical recipe, all code paths, G1\\u2013G4, scorer unit test, first p_d and \\u015d_run estimates, LR smoke test, memory-scaling sanity. Analytic cost ~2.8 node-hours training + ~0.2 eval on an 8\\u00d7H100-class node.\\n- **Gate G-A (fidelity):** G1\\u2013G4 pass \\u2192 proceed. Fail on G3 \\u2192 proceed **accuracy-only** with a mechanism-fidelity-failure label and no cost claims. Fail on G4 \\u2192 halt, re-pin implementation.\\n- **Gate G-B (scorer):** \\u226595 % agreement with 200 hand labels \\u2192 freeze eval harness. Fail \\u2192 fix scorer; score nothing.\\n\\n**Tier 1 \\u2014 minimum-viable 70B.** Arms A + B-md only, headline LR, single \\u03c1, **k=2 seeds** for variance/memory estimation (not for claims). Analytic: ~4.1 h per 70B epoch on 8\\u00d7H100-class at MFU 0.40; ~15 h on A100-class at MFU 0.35.\\n- **Gate G-C:** runs complete without divergence and \\u015d_run is estimated \\u2192 **unlocks k \\u2208 {3,5,7}** via \\u00a77 and the \\u03c1_um choice.\\n- **Gate G-D (power reality check):** compare locked achieved resolution with the MRD; if resolution > 1.5 pt the study is flagged *before unblinding* as unable to certify a 0.5 pt difference, and this is written into the report.\\n\\n**Tier 2 \\u2014 full design.** k seeds; parameter-matched arms; update-matched arm B-um; \\u03c1 \\u2208 {1/16,1/8,1/4} curve; r=64 sensitivity; LR grid; few-shot sensitivity; exploratory battery.\\n\\n**Cost matrix (analytic estimates, NOT measurements; MFU and decode efficiency are assumptions).**\\n\\n| Configuration | Training node-h | Eval node-h | Eval share |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 8\\u00d7H100-class, k=3, 1 epoch, 2 arms | 24.8 | 3.9 (MFU_dec 0.25) \\u2026 9.8 (0.10) | 14\\u201328 % |\\n| 8\\u00d7H100-class, k=5 | 41.3 | 6.2 \\u2026 15.5 | 13\\u201327 % |\\n| 8\\u00d7A100-class, k=3 | 89.7 | 12.4 \\u2026 31.0 | 12\\u201326 % |\\n| 8\\u00d7A100-class, k=5 | 149.6 | 19.6 \\u2026 49.0 | 12\\u201325 % |\\n| Tier 0 (8B) | ~2.8 | ~0.2 | ~7 % |\\n\\n**Eval-serving is a first-class line item (T3):** the zero-shot battery generates \\u22489.5 M tokens per model (22.4 M with the 4/8-shot sensitivity) \\u2014 \\u22480.16 B generated tokens across 7 models (2 arms \\u00d7 3 seeds + base), i.e. 0.59 of one 70B training epoch in FLOPs and 1.4\\u201312 node-hours depending on serving efficiency. **Decode-bound serving can therefore consume a quarter to a third of the total budget**, and is budgeted explicitly rather than discovered late; note that 70B bf16 weights (~140 GB) force a multi-GPU serving replica for evaluation regardless of arm.\\n**Update-matched cost law (T6):** `C_match \\u2248 C_LoRA \\u00b7 (1/\\u03c1) \\u00b7 \\u03c4`, \\u03c4 = BAdam step time / LoRA step time. At \\u03c1 = 1/8 with \\u03c4 \\u2265 0.5 this is \\u22654\\u00d7 LoRA, which is why \\u03c1_um = 1/2 is pre-registered; this arm doubles as the honest compute-matched comparison and directly tests the efficiency claim.\\n**Storage:** base weights 140 GB per run; r=16 adapter 417 MB bf16 plus ~2.5 GB fp32 master + moments; r=64 optimizer moments 6.63 GB all-active vs 0.83 GB at 1/8 active (T7) \\u2014 the reason any large memory win must be attributed to activations/backprop, and hence why G3 is a gate.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Results / reporting template (null and memory-only outcomes reportable by construction)\\n\\n```\\nT1 Accuracy (primary): arm \\u00d7 seed \\u00d7 {GSM8K, MATH} accuracy, extraction-failure %,\\n   format-failure %, \\u0394-from-base; full and decontaminated populations labelled.\\nT2 Co-primary secondary: parameter-matched vs update-matched \\u0394\\u0302 with 95 % CI.\\nT3 Cost (descriptive): peak memory by component, tokens/s, step time, wall-clock,\\n   GPU-h, checkpoint bytes, IO volume, EVAL-SERVING node-hours.\\nT4 Mechanism fidelity: G1 active fraction (target vs measured), G2 Adam-state\\n   allocation, G3 activation-memory/backprop-node scaling vs \\u03c1, G4 LoRA-equivalence.\\nT5 Contamination: overlap rate per benchmark, retained n, population decision taken.\\nT6 \\u03c1-Pareto: (\\u03c1, accuracy, peak memory, tokens/s) \\u2014 the methodological artifact.\\nF1 Loss/accuracy-vs-step curves, both arms, all seeds.  F2 Pareto frontier.\\n```\\nPre-written verdict sentences:\\n- **Q1 accuracy-win + cost-win:** \\\"BAdam improved zero-shot math accuracy by \\u0394\\u0302 = ___ pt (95 % CI ___, ___), exceeding the pre-registered ___ pt threshold, corroborated in the update-matched regime (\\u0394_um = ___). It also used ___ % less peak memory at equal parameter budget.\\\"\\n- **Q2 accuracy-win + cost-neutral/worse:** \\\"Accuracy improved by \\u0394\\u0302 = ___ (CI ___), corroborated/uncorroborated by the update-matched arm; the memory/throughput advantage did not materialise (\\u0394mem = ___ %, \\u0394tokens/s = ___ %), so BAdam's cost rationale is not supported here.\\\"\\n- **Q3 accuracy-null + cost-win:** \\\"No accuracy difference was detectable at the achieved resolution of ___ pt (\\u0394\\u0302 = ___, CI ___). BAdam used ___ % less peak memory. **Cost reduction was observed; better performance was not.**\\\"\\n- **Q4 accuracy-null + cost-neutral:** \\\"Neither hypothesis was supported at the achieved resolution; the arms are statistically indistinguishable and comparable in cost.\\\"\\n- **Undertraining pattern (mandatory wording when it occurs):** \\\"Parameter-matched BAdam trails LoRA by ___ pt while the update-matched arm reaches parity (\\u0394_um = ___). This is evidence that the deficit follows from the reduced number of active-parameter updates, not from a harmful sparsity mechanism; it is not evidence that BAdam is worse.\\\"\\n- **Fidelity failure:** \\\"Instrumentation showed backward-graph truncation was not realised (T4). The memory/throughput hypothesis could not be tested; only a sparsity-of-updates accuracy comparison is reported.\\\"\\n- **Contamination fallback:** \\\"Overlap removal would have retained only ___ % of items, below the pre-registered 80 % floor; the primary population is therefore the full test set with overlap rate ___ % disclosed.\\\"\\n- **Seed insufficiency:** \\\"With k = ___ and \\u015d_run = ___ pt this study resolves differences of \\u2248___ pt; a null is a statement about resolution, not equivalence.\\\"\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Assumption A-list, with breakage mode\\n\\n| # | Assumption (NOT an established fact) | How this design breaks if wrong |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| A1 | Llama 3 70B **base** is the starting point | With Instruct, absolute accuracies and prompt/format handling change; the arm-vs-arm contrast likely transfers but the base-reference floor and extraction profile do not. |\\n| A2 | \\\"Zero-shot\\\" = 0 exemplars, greedy, no CoT trigger, no tools/self-consistency | A different convention (0-shot-CoT, report-style prompts) changes the metric; conclusions may not transfer. |\\n| A3 | BAdam = low-rank update + blocked BCD with inactive blocks **excluded from backprop** (Sun et al., ICLR 2024). **Unverified recollection:** the public BAdam repository reportedly had an early correctness issue fixed after release \\u2014 *unverified, to be checked against the pinned source, never used as a finding.* | If backprop is not truncated, the memory/throughput hypothesis is untestable \\u2192 G3 fails, cost section void. If the pinned implementation differs from the paper, G4 fails \\u2192 comparison invalid until re-pinned. This is exactly why the fidelity probe is a gate. |\\n| A4 | Parameter-matched primary; update-matched corroborates | If the community norm is compute-matched primary, the primary/secondary labels invert and the H1 claim loses standing. |\\n| A5 | MathInstruct = full ~240k pairs, 1 epoch, no resampling | Fewer tokens/epoch scales all costs down but may leave both arms undertrained, weakening any null conclusion. |\\n| A6 | Only linear-projection adapters; no IA\\u00b3/prefix/DoRA/full-finetune arm | A reviewer can argue the baseline is weak relative to stronger adapters; no data here answer that. |\\n| A7 | GSM8K+MATH primary; MATH500 dev-only | A different required benchmark list changes the endpoint post hoc \\u2014 a pre-registration violation. |\\n| A8 | \\u03c1 headline from paper default, else MathInstruct-dev knee | Choosing \\u03c1 using any benchmark would inflate one arm and void the comparison. |\\n| A9 | MFU 0.35\\u20130.40 training, decode efficiency 0.10\\u20130.25 | All node-hour figures shift proportionally; an under-budgeted eval phase can stop Tier 2 mid-sweep. |\\n| A10 | Weights and MathInstruct are obtainable/licensable in the executing environment | Nothing runs; the design stays unexecuted (correctly \\u2014 nothing has run here). |\\n| A11 | \\u015d_run \\u22721 pt, p_d \\u22720.10\\u20130.20 | If larger, the locked resolution exceeds the 0.5 pt MRD and the study can only report equivalence/undetermined \\u2014 by design, not failure. |\\n| A12 | Gradient checkpointing and sharding identical across arms | Otherwise memory comparisons are meaningless. |\\n| A13 | Symbolic-equivalence scoring \\u2248 human grading | Systematic extraction bias would distort accuracy and differentially penalise one arm's output style; mitigated by Gate G-B and per-arm extraction-failure reporting. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. External-validity note (practitioner starting from Instruct)\\nA practitioner will almost always start from `Llama-3-70B-Instruct`. There, absolute accuracies are far higher, so the zero-shot floor-artefact risk largely disappears and the few-shot sensitivity arm matters less; the input distribution is already chat-formatted, so the adapter's job is narrower and lower-capacity, which plausibly **shrinks** any capacity-limited gap between arms and compresses both toward a ceiling \\u2014 while the cost axis is unchanged or worse, since chat templates are longer. Two effects could flip the practical conclusion: an instruct model has fewer headroom points, making a null the expected outcome, and finetuning can *degrade* an already-strong instruct model, promoting extraction/format-failure rate from diagnostic to first-order outcome. This design's arm-vs-arm contrast should transfer qualitatively, but its absolute accuracies, the appropriateness of its 0.5 pt MRD, and its null-vs-regression reading should not be quoted for an instruct starting point without a dedicated run.\\n\\n## 12. Citation defensibility\\nMechanism-level attribution only: **LoRA** (Hu et al., 2021) \\u2014 frozen base weights plus trainable low-rank pairs on selected linear projections, all trainable parameters updated every step. **BAdam** (Sun et al., ICLR 2024) \\u2014 blocked block-coordinate descent over low-rank parameters, a subset activated per step, advertised for memory-efficient finetuning of large models. I verified **no** repository, paper text, hyperparameter table, or default \\u03c1 in this environment; every version-dependent detail (default block ratio, \\u03b1 convention, whether backprop is truncated, the state of the early public release) must be checked against the pinned source before Gate G-A and is flagged unverified in A3.\\n\\n## 13. Pre-registration artifacts to freeze before any run\\nConfig bundle + hashes; \\u03c1 grid and headline-selection rule; LR grid and dev-slice selection rule; seed list; contamination thresholds and population rule; scorer version + 200-item hand-label set; analysis script with locked estimators, MRD/resolution procedure and quadrant sentences; fidelity-gate pass criteria \\u2014 all frozen before 70B outcomes are unblinded.\", \"evidence\": \"**E \\u2014 direct sandbox probes (exploratory environment checks only; no experiment, no training, no download)**\\n\\n- **E1 Workspace is empty of research inputs.** `ls -la /workspace` and `find /workspace -maxdepth 3` \\u2192 single file `task.json` (613 B). `cat /workspace/task.json` contains only the question, \\\"Do not claim that an experiment was run\\\", and `documents.track.md: \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"` No repository, no source code, no data.\\n- **E2 No ML stack, no datasets, no reference implementations.** `python3 -V` \\u2192 3.14.4. `import torch`, `import transformers`, `import peft` \\u2192 all `ModuleNotFoundError`. `pip list` shows only system packages. `find / -maxdepth 3` for `*mathinstruct*`, `*llama*3*`, `*badam*`, `*lora*` \\u2192 no matches. No `~/.cache` and no `~/.cache/huggingface`. **MathInstruct, Llama 3-70B weights, and any BAdam/LoRA reference implementation are NOT locally available; nothing was downloaded or run.**\\n- **E3 No usable GPU.** `/usr/bin/nvidia-smi` exists but prints \\\"NVIDIA-SMI has failed because it couldn't communicate with the NVIDIA driver\\\"; `ls /dev/nvidia*` \\u2192 no device nodes (only `/dev/nvidia-caps`); `lspci` lists host GB100-class 3D controllers that are **not exposed** into this bubblewrap sandbox; task spec states `gpu_enabled: false`.\\n- **E4 No network.** `socket.create_connection(('pypi.org',443))` \\u2192 `socket.gaierror: Temporary failure in name resolution`. Consistent with `network: disabled`; no install or download possible. (Python 3.14 is a secondary practical blocker for CUDA wheels.)\\n- **E5 Irrelevant host resources.** `nproc` = 224; `free -g` \\u2248 2011 GB RAM; `/` and `/tmp` are tmpfs ~1006 GB with 0 used. CPU/RAM cannot substitute for the accelerator training requires.\\n\\n**T \\u2014 analytic arithmetic (formulas + inputs stated; these are estimates, NOT measurements)**\\n\\n- **T1 Training volume.** 240 000 samples \\u00d7 ~700 tokens = 1.68\\u00d710\\u2078 tokens/epoch; 4\\u00b7P\\u00b7T with P=70\\u00d710\\u2079 \\u2192 **4.704\\u00d710\\u00b9\\u2079 FLOPs per 70B epoch** \\u2192 ~4.1 h on an 8\\u00d7H100-class node (peak 989 TFLOP/s/GPU, MFU 0.40) and ~15.0 h on an 8\\u00d7A100-80G-class node (312 TFLOP/s, MFU 0.35). k seeds \\u00d7 2 arms: k=3 \\u2192 24.8 node-h (H100-class) / 89.7 (A100-class); k=5 \\u2192 41.3 / 149.6.\\n- **T2 Decode/eval model.** Generation FLOPs = 2\\u00b7P\\u00b7(prompt+output tokens) per item; zero-shot battery (MATH 5000, GSM8K 1319, GSM-Hard 1319, SVAMP 1000, MultiArith 600, MMLU-math 1800, AQuA 254, TheoremQA 800) = 1.757\\u00d710\\u00b9\\u2078 FLOPs per model; adding 4- and 8-shot on the two primaries = 3.993\\u00d710\\u00b9\\u2078 per model; \\u00d77 models (2 arms \\u00d7 3 seeds + frozen base) = 2.795\\u00d710\\u00b9\\u2079.\\n- **T3 Eval-serving as a first-class cost.** = 0.59 of one 70B training epoch in FLOPs. Token-volume cross-check: 9.49 M generated tokens per model zero-shot, 22.43 M with the few-shot sensitivity, 0.157 B across 7 models \\u2192 1.4\\u20135.5 node-hours at 4000\\u20131000 aggregate tokens/s/GPU on 8 GPUs; FLOPs-based with MFU_dec 0.25 \\u2192 3.9 (H100-class) / 12.4 (A100-class), and 9.8 / 31.0 at MFU_dec 0.10. Eval share of the total budget: 12\\u201328 %. Raising max_new_tokens 1024\\u21922048 multiplies long-output sets by ~1.67. Serving needs \\u2265140 GB of bf16 weights \\u2192 multi-GPU replica irrespective of arm.\\n- **T4 Seed sizing (used in \\u00a77 table).** Seed-term half-width = t_{k\\u22121}\\u00b7\\u015d_run/\\u221ak (t\\u208ddf2\\u208e=4.303, df3=3.182, df4=2.776, df6=2.447, df8=2.365). For \\u015d_run=0.8/1.2/2.0 pt and k=3/4/5/7/9 the smallest claimable \\u0394\\u0302 over a 0.5 pt MRD ranges 1.13\\u21925.47 pt; e.g. \\u015d_run=1.2: k=3 \\u2192 3.48 pt, k=5 \\u2192 1.99 pt, k=7 \\u2192 1.61 pt.\\n- **T5 Item-level floor and contamination power.** Paired item SE on the pooled endpoint = 0.5\\u00b7\\u221a(p_d/n_g + p_d/n_m) with n_g=1319, n_m=5000 \\u2192 half-width 0.68 pt (p_d=0.05), 0.96 (0.10), 1.17 (0.15), 1.36 (0.20), 1.66 (0.30). Filtering both sets to retained fraction f: at p_d=0.20, f=0.8 \\u2192 1.52 pt, f=0.6 \\u2192 1.75, f=0.4 \\u2192 2.15; at p_d=0.10, f=0.8 \\u2192 1.07, f=0.6 \\u2192 1.24, f=0.4 \\u2192 1.52. This is the arithmetic basis for the pre-registered \\u22650.80 retention / n_GSM8K \\u2265 1050 / n_MATH \\u2265 4000 floor (\\u2264~1.5 pt half-width even at p_d=0.20) and the revert-to-full-set fallback.\\n- **T6 Update-matching cost law.** C_match \\u2248 C_LoRA \\u00b7 (1/\\u03c1) \\u00b7 \\u03c4 with \\u03c4 = BAdam step time / LoRA step time; at \\u03c1=1/8, \\u03c4\\u22650.5 \\u21d2 \\u22654\\u00d7 LoRA cost \\u2014 the stated reason \\u03c1_um = 1/2 is pre-registered for the co-primary secondary arm.\\n- **T7 Parameter/memory arithmetic from Llama-3-70B geometry** (80 layers, d=8192, kv-head total dim 1024, ffn 28672, 7 target linears/block; per-block \\u03a3(in+out)=162 832): r=8 \\u2192 103.5 M trainable (0.148 %), r=16 \\u2192 207.1 M (0.296 %), r=32 \\u2192 414.2 M (0.592 %), r=64 \\u2192 828.4 M (1.183 %) of 70 B. All-active fp32 Adam moments at r=64 \\u2248 6.63 GB vs \\u22480.83 GB at 1/8 active fraction; bf16 70B weights = 140 GB; r=16 adapter = 417 MB bf16, +2.5 GB fp32 master + moments. Because optimizer-state savings are single-digit GB, any large memory win must come from activation/backprop truncation \\u2014 which is why G3 is a hard gate rather than a footnote.\\n- **T8 Tier-0 pilot sizing.** 8B training scales by P: 3 seeds \\u00d7 2 arms \\u00d7 1 epoch \\u2248 2.83 node-h and \\u22480.2 node-h eval on an 8\\u00d7H100-class node (same formulas as T1/T2 with P=8\\u00d710\\u2079); 70B/8B compute ratio \\u2248 8.75\\u00d7.\\n\\n**Repository observation.** There is no repository to integrate with: the entire task surface is a single 613-byte JSON containing the question and a design-only note. Consequently the design specifies its own toolchain requirements (pinned BAdam and PEFT implementations, FSDP/ZeRO-3 trainer, vLLM/HF-serving-style batched greedy decoding for eval) as artifacts to be created and hash-frozen at Tier 0, rather than modifications to existing code \\u2014 and every implementation-dependent quantity (default \\u03c1, \\u03b1 convention, backprop truncation) is deferred to the pinned-source verification step instead of being asserted.\", \"assumptions\": [\"A1 The finetuning starting point is the Llama 3 70B **base** checkpoint (design directive); with an Instruct start, absolute accuracies, prompt handling and the null-vs-regression reading change (\\u00a711).\", \"A2 'Zero-shot' is fixed as: zero in-context exemplars, one neutral instruction, greedy decoding, no CoT trigger, no tools, no self-consistency, no answer-checker reranking.\", \"A3 BAdam is attributed a LoRA-style low-rank update trained by blocked block-coordinate descent with an \\u03c1 fraction of low-rank parameters active per step, and \\u2014 the load-bearing engineering assumption \\u2014 inactive blocks excluded from the backward graph. The recollection of an early post-release correctness fix in the public BAdam repo is explicitly UNVERIFIED and is to be checked against the pinned source; it is tied to gates G3/G4 and is never used as a finding.\", \"A4 Parameter-matched is the primary comparison; update-volume-matched is the co-primary secondary that must corroborate or explain the primary.\", \"A5 MathInstruct is used as ~240k pairs for 1 epoch with no resampling or filtering; the token-volume arithmetic scales linearly if this changes.\", \"A6 Only linear-projection adapters are compared; no IA3/prefix/DoRA/full-finetune arm (full finetuning of 70B is assumed out of budget).\", \"A7 GSM8K test + MATH test are the primary zero-shot endpoints, MATH500 is development-only, and GSM-Hard/SVAMP/MultiArith/MMLU-math/AQuA/TheoremQA are exploratory.\", \"A8 No GPU class, node count, MFU or GPU-hour envelope is assumed: all cost figures are parameterised by (GPU class x count x MFU) placeholders to be replaced by Tier-0 measurements.\", \"A9 Training and decode MFU placeholders are 0.35-0.40 and 0.10-0.25 respectively; average sample length ~700 tokens; average prompt ~200-350 tokens with 1024-token generation caps.\", \"A10 Llama 3-70B weights and MathInstruct are obtainable and licensable in some hypothetical executing environment; nothing is available or downloaded here.\", \"A11 Achievable statistical resolution is treated as an empirical quantity estimated only from Tier 0/Tier 1 (arm-blind) and locked before 70B outcomes are unblinded; the 0.5 pt MRD is never widened post hoc.\", \"A12 Gradient checkpointing, sharding plan, batch size in tokens and sequence length are identical across arms, so peak-memory comparisons are interpretable.\", \"A13 Symbolic-equivalence grading approximates human judgement; extraction failures count as incorrect and are reported per arm.\", \"A14 Mechanism-level attributions to Hu et al. 2021 and Sun et al. ICLR 2024 are at the level defensible from general knowledge; no paper text, repository, or hyperparameter table was inspected here.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Statistical resolution, not effort, is the binding constraint: with 1319+5000 test items the paired item-level term alone gives a ~1.0-1.7 pt half-width, so a 0.5 pt MRD may be unclaimable at ANY seed count unless observed arm discordance is low. This is disclosed pre-unblinding rather than resolved by more compute.\", \"The true run-level SD (s_run) of the pooled endpoint is unknown; if it exceeds ~1.5 pt the pre-registered arm-blind seed addition triggers and Tier 2 cost rises ~40-60%.\", \"Whether the pinned BAdam implementation actually truncates backprop for inactive blocks (Gate G3). If not, the entire cost hypothesis is untestable and the study silently becomes an accuracy-only sparsity comparison.\", \"The default per-step active-block ratio in the reference implementation, and whether the paper's reported configuration is reproducible; this determines the headline rho and therefore the headline result.\", \"The unverified recollection of an early correctness issue in the public BAdam release; if true and the pinned commit predates the fix, results would be invalid until re-pinned (Gate G4 exists to detect this).\", \"Actual MathInstruct-to-benchmark overlap rates are unmeasured; if retention falls below the 0.80 floor the primary population reverts to the full test set and contamination must be disclosed in the headline.\", \"Serving throughput for 70B greedy decoding (1.4-12+ node-hours across configurations) may exceed budget and truncate the exploratory battery or the few-shot sensitivity arm.\", \"Licensing/download feasibility for Llama 3-70B weights and MathInstruct in any executing environment is unconfirmed; no execution has occurred or is claimed.\", \"Sensitivity to sequence length (2k vs 4k), packing policy, and gradient-checkpointing granularity is unquantified and can shift both the accuracy and the memory conclusions.\", \"Whether reviewers treat cost metrics as purely descriptive (as designed) or expect them to enter the decision rule; the quadrant scheme keeps the two separate either way.\"], \"stage\": \"result\"}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
